{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791374266,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610071957290725d9b794384c83\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me start by reading the relevant classes. The bug is about parsing responses with multiple tool calls where the source contains a single opening brace in a string literal. The JSON parsing is likely in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL or ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP.\\n\\nThe symptom: arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}). That suggests that when searching for the end of the JSON arguments object, the parser counts braces, but a string literal containing '{' inside the source string messes up brace counting. Actually, the extra text \\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"} suggests the parser found the end of the arguments object too late or too early... Let's think.\\n\\nThe tool call looks like:\\n```json\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"},\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nWhen parsing the \\\"arguments\\\" string, the parser must find the closing brace of the arguments object embedded in a string. If it does brace counting naively on the raw text (not accounting for braces inside string literals), the '{' inside the string literal would increment depth and the parser would then continue past the real end, ending at the '}' of ... hmm, the extra text is `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. That means the parsed arguments string was `{...}` plus `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 i.e., the parser ended the arguments object at the final `}` of the whole tool call object instead of the correct inner `}`. Wait \u2014 the arguments value is a string containing escaped JSON: `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\"`. So the raw text has `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 hmm, actually within the arguments string, the inner JSON quotes are escaped as \\\\\\\", but braces are not escaped. So a '{' inside the source string literal appears as a raw '{' character in the arguments string. If the parser finds the arguments JSON by scanning for matching braces from the opening '{' after `\\\"arguments\\\":`, counting raw '{' and '}' characters, the '{' inside the string literal would make depth go to 2, and then... the escaped quotes contain `}`? No. Let me think about where the end would land.\\n\\nActually more likely: the parser extracts the arguments object substring by brace matching: start at '{' after \\\"arguments\\\":, count depth, end when depth returns to 0. With the extra '{' inside a string, the depth never returns to 0 at the real end '}' (depth goes 1 -\\u003e ... -\\u003e at the inner real closing '}' depth would be 1, not 0)... wait the '{' inside string adds +1, so at real closing brace depth becomes 1 instead of 0, so scanning continues until the '}' that closes the outer tool-call object, where depth becomes 0. So the extracted arguments would include `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 exactly the symptom! And then the second call is lost because the scanner consumed the rest of the response.\\n\\nSo the bug is in the tool-call parsing in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (or JSON_UTIL) \u2014 brace matching that ignores string literals / escapes.\\n\\nLet me read the classes. Start with ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610071957290725d9b794384c83\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by reading the classes involved in parsing LLM responses and tool calls.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_202610071957290725d9b794384c83\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228018432581560388\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228018432581560388\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228018432581560387\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228018432581560387\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":899,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":896},\"output_tokens\":794,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":740},\"total_tokens\":1693}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791374275,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007195746f7effce787ca4a68\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now let's read the JSON util class, since the bug is likely in extract_balanced or split_entries: a string literal containing an opening brace '{' would increment the depth counter inside extract_balanced, or split_entries would split incorrectly... Actually the bug: arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}) and the second call is lost. That suggests extract_balanced for key 'arguments' with open '{' close '}' didn't stop at the correct closing brace because the string literal '{' incremented depth. Wait, but the arguments are a JSON string, i.e. \\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\". The '{' inside the escaped string literal. In extract_balanced, they probably ignore string context, so the '{' inside the string increments depth, and then closing '}' of the entry decrements... leading to capturing more text: the balanced substring extends past the arguments value to the end of the object, giving extra text ',\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}' minus last brace. And split_entries for the second call: since the array splitting may also be confused? Actually the second call is lost likely because split_entries splits at depth 0 at commas... hmm.\\n\\nLet's read the JSON util.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007195746f7effce787ca4a68\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011904231272151\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011904231272151\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3787,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":3776},\"output_tokens\":291,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":272},\"total_tokens\":4078}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791374485,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_2026100719575578bf18ea19f74aa3\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now I understand the cause. Both `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` count raw braces without being string-aware. If a string literal inside the JSON contains `{` (e.g., the ABAP source `lv_open = '{'.` inside the escaped `arguments` string), the brace depth counter is thrown off:\\n\\n- `extract_balanced` with key `arguments`, open `{`, close `}`: for responses API, arguments is a JSON *string*, e.g. `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. Hmm, extract_balanced looks for `\\\"arguments\\\"` then the first `{` after it. If the arguments value is a string starting with `{\\\"source\\\":...`, the first `{` is at the beginning of the JSON-string-escaped object. The depth counting then counts braces inside the string literal too. With a single extra `{` inside a string, the depth never returns to 0 at the true end; it closes one `{` later, i.e., swallows `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` and stops there (the closing `}` of that later part). Actually the report says arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON: `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Yes: the balanced scan runs past the real end of arguments object because one `{` inside the string consumed one `}` at the end. So the extracted arguments include extra trailing `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` and the closing `}` of the entry.\\n\\n- `split_entries` similarly splits top-level `{...}` entries; with an extra `{` inside the first entry's string, the entry's end is found too late \u2014 the first entry swallows the rest until the second entry's closing brace balances. That means the second entry is lost (merged into first or partially). Actually the report says the second call is lost.\\n\\nSo the fix: make brace matching string-aware \u2014 skip over JSON string literals (i.e., when a `\\\"` is encountered, scan to the closing unescaped `\\\"` and continue). Both in `split_entries` and `extract_balanced`.\\n\\nLet me think about the actual JSON shapes.\\n\\nChat API response body:\\n```json\\n{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}\\n```\\n\\nparse_calls_chat: extract_balanced(iv_json=body, key='tool_calls', open='[', close=']'). The array scan for `[`/`]` \u2014 brackets inside strings? Unlikely but possible. The brace inside the string doesn't affect bracket counting. OK so the array extract works.\\n\\nThen split_entries on the array: it counts `{` and `}` ignoring strings. The first entry: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}`. Count: `{` at start =1, `{` of function =2, then `{` inside string =3, ... `}` characters: the escaped braces in string are `\\\\{`? No \u2014 in JSON, `{` inside a string doesn't need escaping. Only `\\\"` and `\\\\`. So the string content contains raw `'{'`. Hmm, wait: the arguments value is a JSON string containing the escaped JSON of the arguments object: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. Inside that string, the source code contains `lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 a literal `{` character. When PIA escaped the source into the arguments string, `{` is not escaped by JSON escape (only `\\\"` `\\\\` controls). So the string contains `... lv_open = '{'. ...`.\\n\\nIn split_entries for the chat array: depth counting. Entry 1: depth 1 at `{`, +1 at `function {` = 2, +1 at the `'{'` inside the string = 3, then `-1` at `\\\"`} closing string... let me just trust the analysis: one unmatched `{` inside a string shifts the perceived close by one `}` later. The entry then ends at the `}` that actually closes... entry2's inner or outer. Then arguments extract similarly swallows trailing `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Note \\\"call_id\\\" and \\\"status\\\" \u2014 that's the *responses* format! `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` appears in responses API output items:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nSo the bug report is about parse_calls_responses (api_type = 'responses'). The output array:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"output\\\":[\\n {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},\\n {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n]\\n```\\n\\nWait but arguments is a string in responses too. So in the entry, after `\\\"arguments\\\"` there's a string `\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...\\\"`. extract_balanced(key='arguments', open='{', close='}') finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` then the first `{` \u2014 which is inside the string (`{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"...`). Good, that's the start of the arguments object (as escaped JSON string). Depth counting: `{`=1 (from `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"`), then the literal `'{'` in the source = 2. Then `}` chars: end of arguments object `\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2192 the `}` there decrements to 1 (doesn't close!), then `,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 that final `}` decrements to 0. So rv_ = `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 extra text after the JSON, exactly matching the report: `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})` \u2014 well, they mention call_id too; order may vary. Yes matches.\\n\\nAlso split_entries: the first `{...}` entry counting braces: entry1 has `{` at start(1), `{` in source string(2), `}` at end of arguments string(\u21921), `}` closing entry1(\u21920). Hmm wait \u2014 entry1 also has the `}` at the end: `\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Let me count: braces in entry1: `{` start; `{` inside source string; `}` closing the arguments object inside the string; `}` at end of entry. So depth: 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 1 \u2192 0. That actually balances! Because the extra `{` inside the string is compensated by... hmm: `{`=1, `{`(string)=2, `}`(string, end of args obj)=1, `}`(entry end)=0. So split_entries ends the entry at the right place? Wait, but there's also the closing of nothing... Let me re-examine: the arguments string contains `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 that's `{` at the start of the args object and `}` at the end. Plus the literal `'{'` in the source. So string contributes +2 `{` and +1 `}` = net +1. Entry structure contributes +1 `{` (start) and -1 `}` (end) = net 0. Total net +1 \u2192 doesn't balance within entry1. The scan continues into entry2: entry2 has `{`(start)=+1... wait depth currently 1 after entry1's final `}`? Let me recount sequentially:\\n\\nEntry1 text: `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n\\n- `{` \u2192 depth 1\\n- `{` (inside string, args object start) \u2192 2\\n- `'{'` literal in source \u2192 3\\n- `}` (end of args object, inside string) \u2192 2\\n- `}` (end of entry1) \u2192 1\\n\\nDepth never reaches 0. Scan continues into `,` and entry2: `{` \u2192 2, `{` (args object in string) \u2192 3, `}` \u2192 2, `}` (end entry2) \u2192 1. Then hits `]`... loop ends when lv_pos reaches end with depth\\u003e0 \u2192 lv_end = len-1, so the whole rest becomes ONE entry. Hence: only one entry, containing both calls; the first call's arguments swallow trailing text and the second call is lost. Exactly the reported symptom.\\n\\nSo the fix: string-aware brace/bracket matching in both `split_entries` and `extract_balanced`. When encountering `\\\"` (that begins a string literal at the current structural position), skip to the closing unescaped `\\\"`. Also need to be careful: in extract_balanced, once we find the open char, we scan; strings inside are skipped. In split_entries, same.\\n\\nNow, note the arguments extraction in parse_calls_responses/parse_calls_chat: arguments is a JSON string whose first char is `{` (escaped JSON). extract_balanced finds the first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"`. With string-aware scanning, the `{` inside the string is still found by the `FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open` (that FIND isn't string-aware, it just finds the first `{`). Then we scan with string awareness: at position lv_start we have `{` (depth 1). Next chars are `\\\\` `\\\"` etc. Hmm, but the scanning loop must know we're inside a JSON string? No \u2014 from the perspective of raw text, the arguments value is a sequence of characters: `{`, `\\\\`, `\\\"`, `s`, ... The brace matcher on this raw text: at `{` depth=1. Then it encounters `\\\\` and `\\\"` \u2014 a naive string-aware scanner sees `\\\"` and thinks a string starts! That would skip until the next unescaped `\\\"`, which is... `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"` \u2014 after `\\\\` comes `\\\"`, the scanner at `\\\\`... hmm.\\n\\nLet me think carefully. String-aware JSON scanning works on the *outer* JSON structure. In the outer structure, after `\\\"arguments\\\":` comes a string literal starting with `\\\"`. Everything until the closing unescaped `\\\"` is string content. So a properly string-aware scanner that starts before `\\\"arguments\\\"` would treat the whole arguments value as a string and never see `{`/`}` inside it.\\n\\nBut extract_balanced is designed here to *enter* the string: it finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` then the first `{` after it (which is inside the string), and extracts the escaped JSON object from within the string (still escaped, then unescape is applied by callers). So extract_balanced's semantics: extract the balanced-brace span starting at the first open char after the key, in *raw* text.\\n\\nSo within that raw escaped-JSON text, the content is itself JSON-with-escapes. Braces inside string literals of the *inner* JSON are escaped? No! The inner JSON is escaped as a JSON string: the inner JSON's `\\\"` become `\\\\\\\"`, but `{` stays `{`. And the inner JSON's string literals (like the source code) contain `{` unescaped at both levels.\\n\\nSo to correctly match braces of the inner (escaped) JSON, the scanner must handle escaped quotes: `\\\\\\\"` is an escaped quote inside the outer string, which represents a quote of the inner JSON; a string literal of the inner JSON spans from `\\\\\\\"` to `\\\\\\\"`. And a `\\\\\\\\` in outer represents `\\\\` in inner; `\\\\\\\\\\\"` in outer = inner `\\\\` followed by quote \u2192 quote closes... hmm, this gets tricky but manageable.\\n\\nLet me think about what the raw text looks like. Outer JSON body:\\n\\n`\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"`\\n\\nRaw characters after `\\\"arguments\\\":`:\\n`\\\"` `\\\\` `{` `\\\\` `\\\"` `s` `o` `u` `r` `c` `e` `\\\\` `\\\"` `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` `C` `L` ... `'{` `'` `.` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `,` `\\\\` `\\\"` `n` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` `Z` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `}` `\\\"` `,` `\\\"` `s` ...\\n\\nSo the inner JSON's quotes appear as `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`. The inner string literals are delimited by `\\\\\\\"`...`\\\\\\\"`. A brace scanner for this raw text should treat `\\\\\\\"` as a quote delimiter of the inner JSON: i.e., when it sees `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`, that toggles inner-string state? Not exactly: `\\\\\\\"` in the outer representation is the character `\\\"` in the inner JSON. Inner JSON string delimiters are `\\\"` \u2192 represented as `\\\\\\\"` in the raw outer text. So in raw text, inner string literals are delimited by the two-char sequence `\\\\\\\"`. And an inner escaped quote `\\\\\\\"` (inner) becomes `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` in raw (backslash\u2192`\\\\\\\\`, quote\u2192`\\\\` + `\\\"`). So raw `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` = inner `\\\\\\\"`.\\n\\nSo the scanner on raw text:\\n- state: in_inner_string (bool)\\n- when not in string: if we see `\\\\` and next is `\\\"` \u2192 enter string (this is inner `\\\"`). If we see `{` \u2192 depth++, `}` \u2192 depth--. Also a plain `\\\"` (not preceded by active `\\\\`)... can that occur in raw outside inner strings? The outer string content is the entire arguments; after it ends, raw has `\\\"` (closing outer string, unescaped). E.g., `\\\\\\\"}` then `\\\"` closes the outer string. If our scan is still going (depth not 0), we'd encounter that lone `\\\"` \u2014 meaning we've left the inner JSON and are in the outer object's fields. Ideally the brace matcher terminates before that: depth reaches 0 at the inner object's closing `}` which appears as raw `}` right before the outer's closing `\\\"`.\\n\\nHold on: inner `}` is not escaped, so raw `}`. And inner object's closing brace is the last char of the outer string value, followed by `\\\"`. So a correct inner-aware scan stops exactly there: depth 0 at that raw `}`. \\n\\nBut if the source has an extra unbalanced `{`, the naive scan runs past. With inner-string awareness, the `'{'` inside the source is inside an inner string literal (`\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"`), so it's skipped \u2192 depth stays correct \u2192 scan ends at the right `}`.\\n\\nNow, how to implement scanning raw text with inner-string awareness:\\n\\nIterate chars:\\n- If in_string:\\n  - if current char is `\\\\` \u2192 skip next char (escape; covers `\\\\\\\\` and `\\\\\\\"` within the inner string, and `\\\\n` etc.)\\n  - else if current char is `\\\"` \u2192 hmm, within the outer string representation, the inner `\\\"` is `\\\\\\\"`. So in raw, inside an inner string, a `\\\"` is always preceded by `\\\\`. If we handle escapes by skipping 2 chars when seeing `\\\\`, then a lone `\\\"` inside inner string can't occur... Actually within the outer string, every `\\\"` must be escaped as `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 JSON requirement. So raw `\\\"` inside the outer string only appears as part of `\\\\\\\"`. So:\\n\\nScanner on raw text (extract_balanced after finding open char):\\n```\\npos = start of open char; depth = 0\\nin_str = abap_false\\nwhile pos \\u003c len:\\n  ch = text+pos(1)\\n  if in_str:\\n    if ch = `\\\\`: pos += 2; continue.   \\\" escaped char inside inner string\\n    elseif ch = `\\\"`: in_str = false.  \\\" shouldn't happen in a well-formed outer string, but end inner string\\n  else:\\n    if ch = `\\\\` and text+pos+1(1) = `\\\"`: in_str = true; pos += 2; continue.\\n    elseif ch = open: depth++\\n    elseif ch = close: depth--; if depth = 0: end = pos; exit.\\n  pos++\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: when not in_str and we see `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` \u2192 that's an inner string delimiter `\\\"` \u2192 enter string. When in_str and we see `\\\\` followed by anything \u2192 skip 2 (escaped char). When in_str and see `\\\"` alone \u2192 close string (this happens for the outer string's closing quote if we overshoot, or in malformed input).\\n\\nThis handles the standard case. What about `split_entries`? There the text is the array of outer objects; the strings there are real outer JSON strings: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...}\\\"`. For outer-level scanning:\\n- not in string: `\\\"` \u2192 enter string.\\n- in string: `\\\\` \u2192 skip next; `\\\"` \u2192 exit string.\\n- `{`/`}` outside strings adjust depth.\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 inside the outer string, the inner JSON's quotes are `\\\\\\\"`; the outer-scanner sees `\\\\` then skips `\\\"`. Good. The `'{'` inside the outer string is skipped because we're in a string. \\n\\nBut split_entries scanning: when in a string, a `\\\\\\\"` (backslash-quote) is skipped via the `\\\\` escape rule. What about `\\\\\\\\` at the end of a source line (ABAP `\\\\` in code)? Inner source `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 outer raw `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`. Outer scanner: sees `\\\\`, skips next `\\\\`. Fine.\\n\\nBut here's the subtlety for split_entries: it operates on the *outer* JSON level, where strings are delimited by `\\\"`. However, the braces we want to count are the outer object braces `{...}`. The arguments value is a string \u2192 all its content (including `{`, `}`, `\\\\\\\"`) skipped. \\n\\nFor extract_balanced, the scanning is at the *inner* level as described. Two different scanning modes. Hmm, can I unify? For extract_balanced, the raw text after the key: the open `{` we find is inside the outer string (for the arguments case), OR it could be a real structural brace (e.g., key `function` open `{` in the chat format, where arguments is also a string inside; or key `tool_calls` open `[`).\\n\\nHmm, extract_balanced is used with:\\n1. key='tool_calls', open='[', close=']' on the outer body \u2192 structural.\\n2. key='function', open='{', close='}' on an entry (chat) \u2192 structural (the function object).\\n3. key='arguments', open='{', close='}' on an entry \u2192 the value is a string containing escaped JSON \u2192 the open char is inside a string.\\n4. key='output', open='[', close=']' on outer body \u2192 structural.\\n\\nFor case 3, the scanner must handle the `\\\\`+`\\\"` as inner string delimiters. For cases 1,2,4, outer structural scanning: strings delimited by `\\\"`, escapes `\\\\X` skipped.\\n\\nInteresting: can one scanner handle both? Consider raw text of case 3 inner level: delimiters are `\\\\\\\"`. Case outer: delimiters are `\\\"`. If I use the inner-level scanner on outer text: outer `\\\"arguments\\\"` \u2014 at `\\\"`, not preceded by `\\\\`... the scanner's rule \\\"not in_str and ch = `\\\\` and next = `\\\"` \u2192 enter string\\\" wouldn't trigger; a bare `\\\"` (outer string start) with the inner scanner would... not be recognized as a string start (rule only enters on `\\\\\\\"`). Then it would count braces inside the string. Bad.\\n\\nAlternatively, a scanner that enters string mode on ANY `\\\"` when not in string (outer rule), and inside a string skips `\\\\X` pairs and exits on `\\\"`:\\n\\nCase 3 (inner): raw `{\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}`. At pos0: `{` depth 1. Then `\\\"` \u2192 enter string! Then `s,o,u,r,c,e` then `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"` ... then continues in string: `:` `\\\\` skip `\\\"` ... all the way: it stays in string until the next bare `\\\"`. The next bare `\\\"` \u2014 after the args object's closing `}` comes the outer string's closing `\\\"`. So the scanner would treat everything from `{\\\"source...` to the outer close as one string, never seeing the inner `}` \u2192 depth never 0 \u2192 overshoot. Bad.\\n\\nSo we need two modes, or a smarter approach: a scanner that treats `\\\\` + `\\\"` as *both* potentially entering/exiting strings depending on parity... Think: inner-level scanning where strings are delimited by `\\\\\\\"`:\\n\\nRaw: `{` `\\\\` `\\\"` `s` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `,` `\\\\` `\\\"` ... `}` `\\\"`\\n- pos0 `{`: depth 1, not in string.\\n- pos1 `\\\\`, pos2 `\\\"`: this is inner `\\\"` opening a string \u2192 in_str = true.\\n- ... in_str: `\\\\` \u2192 skip next. So `\\\\` `\\\"` (inner closing quote) \u2192 skipped as escape?! That's wrong \u2014 `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` should *close* the inner string.\\n\\nHmm. At the inner level, the inner JSON text is: `{\\\"source\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Z\\\"}`. Its scanning rules: `{` depth++, `\\\"` toggle string, in string `\\\\` skips next char. The raw representation escapes `\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\` and `\\\"`\u2192`\\\\\\\"` for the outer. So if I first *unescape* the raw text and then scan... but extract_balanced returns the still-escaped text (callers unescape afterwards). I could unescape a copy for scanning purposes to find the end offset, then return the raw slice. That's clean!\\n\\nActually wait \u2014 simpler: for extract_balanced, the substring to return is raw (escaped); but for finding the balanced end, I can scan the *logical* (unescaped) inner text. The offsets differ though (each inner `\\\"` is 2 raw chars). Tracking offset mapping is doable but messy.\\n\\nAlternative: implement the inner-level scanner directly on raw text with these rules:\\n\\nState in_str (inner string).\\n- Not in_str:\\n  - `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`: inner string opens \u2192 in_str = true, skip 2. Note: at the inner level, could `\\\\` appear outside a string not followed by `\\\"`? Inner `\\\\` outside a string would be invalid JSON. In raw, inner `\\\\` = `\\\\\\\\`. So raw `\\\\\\\\` outside inner strings shouldn't occur (except invalid JSON). Fine.\\n  - `{`: depth++\\n  - `}`: depth--\\n  - bare `\\\"`: this would be an outer-level quote (i.e., we've left the inner JSON / malformed). Treat as... hmm. For robustness: stop scanning? Or ignore? Let me think about when this happens in practice: if depth never balances within the outer string (e.g., model sent malformed JSON), the scan would continue past the outer string close into `,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` of the outer object and might eventually balance somewhere wrong. With old code that's the observed bug. With new code, the `'{'` case is skipped correctly, so we balance at the right `}`. For malformed input, we might overshoot; adding a guard \\\"bare `\\\"` at outer level while depth\\u003e0 \u2192 keep scanning\\\" \u2014 eh, leave it: treat bare `\\\"` as entering... no. Let me just handle: not in_str, ch = `\\\"` (bare, i.e., not `\\\\`+`\\\"`): this means we're at the outer structural level. For case 3 scanning that shouldn't happen before we've balanced. I could simply ignore bare quotes (count nothing). Actually hmm, there's a subtle case: what if the *inner* JSON contains an escaped quote inside a string: inner `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`. Scanner in_str sees `\\\\` \u2192 skip next (`\\\\`), then sees `\\\\` \u2192 skip next (`\\\"`). Correct: stays in string. \\n\\n- In_str:\\n  - `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`: closes the inner string \u2192 in_str = false, skip 2.\\n  \\n  Wait but that conflicts: in_str, `\\\\` followed by `\\\\` (inner `\\\\\\\\`): raw `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 skip 2, stay in string. in_str, `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`: inner `\\\\\\\"`? No wait \u2014 at inner level, inside a string, `\\\\\\\"` is an escaped quote (stays in string). Raw of inner `\\\\\\\"` is `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"` (4 chars). Hmm I covered that above: skip 2 (`\\\\\\\\`), skip 2 (`\\\\\\\"`). So in_str + raw `\\\\` + `\\\"` \u2014 is that inner `\\\"` closing the string, or part of inner `\\\\\\\"`? Inner string close = inner `\\\"` = raw `\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\`+`\\\"`. Inner escaped quote = inner `\\\\\\\"` = raw `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`. The raw text distinguishes them by the number of preceding backslashes. When scanning in_str and I encounter `\\\\` at pos p with `\\\"` at p+1: is there another `\\\\` at p+2? If raw is `\\\\`,`\\\"`,`\\\"`... can't be \u2014 inner `\\\"` `\\\"` consecutive inside a string would be inner `\\\"` closing then opening, weird. Let me think again:\\n\\n  Inner text possibilities around a `\\\"` inside a string: it must be preceded by `\\\\` (escaped). So inner `\\\\\\\"` = raw `\\\\\\\\` `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 4 raw chars: BS BS BS QUOTE. Inner `\\\"` closing the string: raw `\\\\\\\"` (BS QUOTE) but preceded by whatever the previous inner char was \u2014 if previous inner char was `\\\\` (as literal, raw `\\\\\\\\`), then raw is BS BS BS QUOTE \u2014 ambiguous!! Inner `...\\\\` + closing `\\\"` = inner text `\\\\` then `\\\"` = raw `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = BSBS BSQUOTE \u2014 same 4 chars as inner `\\\\\\\"` escaped quote preceded by nothing? Hmm: inner string `\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"` = chars `\\\"`,`a`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`,`b`,`\\\"`. Raw: `\\\\\\\"`,`a`,`\\\\\\\\`,`\\\\\\\"`,`b`,`\\\\\\\"` \u2192 BSQ a BSBS BSQ b BSQ. Inner string `\\\"a\\\\\\\"` (string containing `a\\\\` then closing quote)? That's `\\\"a\\\\\\\"` \u2014 chars `\\\"`,`a`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`... but JSON inner: `\\\"a\\\\\\\"` \u2014 the `\\\\` escapes the `\\\"`, so the string isn't closed; it's `\\\"a\\\\\\\"...\\\"` i.e. string content `a\\\"...`. To have a string ending in a literal backslash: `\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\"` \u2014 chars `\\\"`,`a`,`\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`. Raw: BSQ a BSBS BSBS BSQ... wait no: inner `\\\\` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\\`; inner `\\\"` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\"`. Inner `\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\"` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` hmm getting confused with double escaping. Inner text: `\\\"` `a` `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\"`. Raw (escape each: `\\\"`\u2192BS Q i.e. `\\\\\\\"`; `\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\`):\\n  - `\\\"` \u2192 `\\\\` `\\\"`\\n  - `a` \u2192 `a`\\n  - `\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\` `\\\\`\\n  - `\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\` `\\\\`\\n  - `\\\"` \u2192 `\\\\` `\\\"`\\n  Raw sequence: BS Q a BS BS BS BS BS Q.\\n\\n  Scanner in_str at `a`, then BS at pos3: with rule \\\"in_str + BS \u2192 skip next char (BS at 4)\\\": pos\u21925. BS at 5: skip BS at 6. pos\u21927: BS Q \u2192 close string. Correct!\\n\\n  Now inner `\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"` (string content `a\\\"b`): raw: BS Q a BS BS BS Q b BS Q. Scanner: in_str, `a`, BS at 3: skip next (BS at 4). pos 5: BS, next is Q at 6 \u2192 close string?! But the string continues with `b`. WRONG.\\n\\n  Hmm. So the naive \\\"in_str + BS \u2192 skip 2\\\" is incorrect for the raw representation of inner text, because inner `\\\\` is raw `\\\\\\\\` (2 chars) and inner `\\\\\\\"` is raw `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"`. The correct approach: at the inner level, `\\\\` skips the next inner char. Raw: `\\\\\\\\` (2 chars) skips... I should scan raw and handle:\\n\\n  in_str rules on raw:\\n  - BS followed by BS: this is inner `\\\\` (an escape start at inner level, consuming the next inner char which is at least 1 char). But inner `\\\\` + what? Inner `\\\\X` where X is any char: raw = BS BS + raw(X). So raw BS BS means \\\"inner escape began\\\"; the escaped char follows (1+ raw chars). If inner X = `\\\"` \u2192 raw X = BS Q \u2192 total BS BS BS Q. If inner X = `n` \u2192 BS BS n.\\n  \\n  So proper algorithm: track at inner level with raw peeking:\\n  - in_str and see BS BS (two backslashes): that's an inner escape `\\\\X`; X begins at pos+2 raw. If raw[pos+2] = BS and raw[pos+3] = Q \u2192 X = `\\\"` \u2192 consume 4, stay in string. Else consume 2 + len(raw(X)). Since X is a normal char (n, t, u+4hex...), u is 6 inner chars = BS BS u X X X X \u2192 hmm inner `\\\\uXXXX`: raw `\\\\\\\\uXXXX`. Consume 2 + 6 = 8. But we don't need exact consumption per escape type; simpler: iterate raw with an inner-level state machine that decodes on the fly:\\n\\n  Simplest robust approach: build the *decoded* inner text progressively while scanning, but track raw offsets. Actually even simpler: since I need the end offset in raw text, I can decode and simultaneously record, for each decoded char, its raw offset... heavy.\\n\\n  Alternative pragmatic approach: instead of scanning raw with inner semantics, do this: find the value string of the key, unescape it, then scan the *unescaped* text for balance (standard JSON scanning: quotes toggle strings, BS inside string skips next char, braces count outside strings). The result is the balanced substring in decoded form. But callers currently do: `unescape( extract_balanced( ... key='arguments' ... ) )`. If extract_balanced returns decoded text for the string-value case, then callers' extra unescape would be harmless? unescape of decoded text: any remaining `\\\\` sequences... decoded inner JSON still has escapes (for the source code quotes etc.). If extract_balanced returns the *decoded-once* text (i.e., the inner JSON text), then applying unescape again would decode the inner JSON's escapes too \u2014 corrupting it (the source string would lose its `\\\\\\\"`...). Hmm wait \u2014 what do callers actually want in ls-arguments?\\n\\n  parse_calls_responses: `ls-arguments = unescape( extract_balanced( iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) )`. The intent: arguments = the JSON object text of the tool arguments (as a JSON string), e.g. `{\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ...\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}` \u2014 where the source content's own special chars remain JSON-escaped (that's the desired end state: arguments is a JSON string that later gets parsed/re-serialized). Currently: extract_balanced returns the raw escaped slice `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...\\\\\\\"}` and unescape decodes the outer escaping \u2192 inner JSON text with its own escapes intact. Correct.\\n\\n  If instead extract_balanced returned the decoded slice directly, callers' unescape would double-decode \u2192 wrong. So I must keep the contract: extract_balanced returns the raw slice; only the *scanning* for the end must be inner-aware.\\n\\n  OK so implement the inner-aware scanner on raw text properly. Let me define it cleanly. The raw text after `\\\"key\\\":` begins (for the arguments case) inside an outer JSON string. The outer string content = inner JSON with `\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\`, `\\\"`\u2192`\\\\\\\"`. Equivalent: raw = escape(inner). I scan raw while maintaining inner-level state (in_inner_string), knowing escape mapping:\\n\\n  Algorithm (scan from position p0 = position of open char; we know raw[p0] = `{` = inner `{`, structural):\\n  ```\\n  depth = 1 (counting the open char)\\n  p = p0 + 1\\n  in_str = false\\n  while p \\u003c len:\\n    if not in_str:\\n      if raw[p] = '{': depth++, p++\\n      elseif raw[p] = '}': depth--, p++; if depth = 0: end found at p-1\\n      elseif raw[p] = '\\\\' and raw[p+1] = '\\\"': in_str = true, p += 2   \\\" inner string open\\n      elseif raw[p] = '\\\"' : \\\" bare quote: outer-level structural quote or malformed; stop? \\n          Actually for robustness just p++ (ignore)\\n      else p++\\n    else \\\" in inner string\\n      if raw[p] = '\\\\' :\\n        if raw[p+1] = '\\\\':  \\\" inner literal backslash (\\\\\\\\): consume both raw chars = one inner char\\n           p += 2\\n           \\\" but wait: inner \\\\\\\\ is one inner char; the NEXT inner char starts at p. If inner next char is \\\", it's raw \\\\\\\" \u2192 string close. Correct because we consumed exactly the two raw chars of the inner backslash.\\n           \\\" Hmm, no! inner `\\\\` is raw `\\\\\\\\`. Consuming BS BS = consumed inner `\\\\` (one inner char). Then continue. Good.\\n           \\\" BUT: inner `\\\\\\\"` (escaped quote, stays in string) = raw `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = BS BS BS Q. With this rule: consume BS BS (the inner `\\\\`), then at BS Q \u2192 string close. WRONG (it's an escaped quote at inner level, string stays open).\\n        endif\\n      ...\\n  ```\\n  Damn, the ambiguity: inner-level escape `\\\\X`. When I see raw BS, the inner char is `\\\\` (an escape introducer at inner level) only if raw BS BS. The inner escape consumes the next inner char X, whose raw form is 1-2 chars (1 if X is not `\\\"` or `\\\\`, 2 if X is `\\\"` or `\\\\`... wait X=`\\\\` would be inner `\\\\\\\\` = raw BS BS BS BS). So:\\n\\n  in_str, raw[p] = BS:\\n    - This BS is the first raw char of inner `\\\\`. The inner escape consumes X = next inner char.\\n    - raw[p+1]: if BS \u2192 X's raw starts at p+2? No...: inner `\\\\` is raw BS BS. So raw[p]=BS raw[p+1]=BS means the inner backslash itself (2 raw chars: p, p+1). Then X (the escaped char) starts at p+2. If X = `\\\"` \u2192 raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=Q. So: if raw[p+2]=BS and raw[p+3]=Q \u2192 consume 4, stay in string. Else consume 2 (the inner backslash) + then X is a simple char at p+2 (for \\\\n, \\\\t, or \\\\uXXXX first char...) \u2014 for \\\\u: X = `u`, then 4 more hex chars; consuming just 2+1=3 is fine because the following chars are ordinary (hex digits) and harmless in scanning.\\n    - If raw[p+1] = Q \u2192 raw BS Q is inner `\\\"` closing the string \u2192 in_str = false, consume 2.\\n    Hmm wait, but that contradicts: in_str, raw[p]=BS raw[p+1]=Q: is that inner `\\\\` + inner X where inner X = `\\\"`? Inner `\\\\` is raw BS BS, not BS. So raw BS Q in-string = inner `\\\"`. Yes! Because raw = escape(inner): any inner `\\\\` becomes BS BS. So a lone BS followed by Q in raw inside a string maps to inner `\\\"`. And raw BS BS maps to inner `\\\\` (which at inner level escapes the next inner char...). \\n\\n    Hmm, but do I need to care that the inner `\\\\` escapes the next char? Consider inner text `a\\\\\\\"b` (string content `a\\\"b`): raw = a BS BS BS Q b. In-string scan: `a`; raw[p]=BS raw[p+1]=BS \u2192 this is inner `\\\\`; the escaped char X: raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=Q \u2192 X = `\\\"` \u2192 consume 4 (BS BS BS Q), stay in string; then `b`. Correct!\\n\\n    Inner text `a\\\\` then closing `\\\"` (string content `a\\\\`): inner = `a` `\\\\` `\\\\`?? No \u2014 to have content ending with a backslash, inner text is `a\\\\\\\\` then `\\\"`. raw: a BS BS BS BS BS Q. Scan: `a`; BS BS \u2192 inner `\\\\`; then raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=BS \u2192 X = `\\\\`?? X's raw: BS BS \u2192 X = `\\\\`. So inner `\\\\\\\\` = raw BS BS BS BS. With my rule: raw[p]=BS raw[p+1]=BS \u2192 inner escape intro; check raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=Q? Here raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=BS \u2192 not Q \u2192 so consume only 2? But the escaped char X=`\\\\` has raw length 2, so I should consume 4. If I consume only 2, then at raw[p+2]=BS raw[p+3]=BS: in_str, BS BS \u2192 inner escape intro again; check raw[p+4]=BS raw[p+5]=Q \u2192 X = `\\\"` \u2192 consume 4, stay in string. WRONG \u2014 that Q closes the string. The string content was `a\\\\` and the Q after BS BS BS BS BS Q closes it... hold on, inner `a\\\\\\\\` + close `\\\"`: inner chars: a, \\\\, \\\\, \\\". Raw: a, BSBS, BSBS, BSQ = a BS BS BS BS BS Q (6 raw after a: BS BS BS BS BS Q \u2014 that's 5 BS + Q? BSBS (2) + BSBS (2) + BSQ (2) = BS BS BS BS BS Q \u2014 yes 5 BS then Q... wait BSBS + BSBS = BS BS BS BS (4 BS), then BSQ = BS Q. Total: BS BS BS BS BS Q? No: BSBS is 2 chars (BS, BS). BSBS + BSBS = 4 chars (BS BS BS BS). + BSQ = BS, Q. Total raw after `a`: BS BS BS BS BS Q \u2192 5 BS + Q. Yes.\\n\\n    Scan: p at BS1: BS BS (BS1 BS2) \u2192 inner escape intro, escaped char X starts at BS3: raw[p+2]=BS3, raw[p+3]=BS4 \u2192 not BS+Q \u2192 consume 2 (BS1 BS2 = the inner `\\\\`??). Hmm, this is where I conflated. Let me redefine precisely:\\n\\n    In-string raw scanning where raw = escape(inner):\\n    - raw BS Q (BS followed by Q) = inner `\\\"` \u2192 close string, consume 2.\\n    - raw BS BS = inner `\\\\`... but inner `\\\\` inside a string is always an escape introducer consuming the next inner char. However, whether it escapes the next char doesn't matter for *string-boundary* scanning IF the next inner char is not `\\\"` or `\\\\`. The problematic combos are exactly: inner `\\\\\\\"` (raw BS BS BS Q) and inner `\\\\\\\\` (raw BS BS BS BS) and inner `\\\\` + `\\\"` closing (raw BS BS BS Q)... wait inner string content ending with escaped backslash then close: inner `...\\\\\\\\` + `\\\"` \u2192 raw ... BS BS BS BS BS Q. And inner `...\\\\\\\"...` (escaped quote mid-string) \u2192 raw ... BS BS BS Q ...\\n\\n    Ugh: raw BSBSBSQ is ambiguous between [inner `\\\\` + inner `\\\"`-close] and [inner `\\\\\\\"` escaped quote]. Indeed at inner level: `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` \u2014 is the `\\\"` escaped (string continues) or... it IS escaped by the `\\\\`. So inner `\\\\\\\"` inside a string = escaped quote, string continues. Inner `\\\\\\\\` = literal backslash. So inner sequence `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"` = literal backslash then closing quote. Raw: BSBS BSBS BSQ = BS BS BS BS BS Q.\\n    And inner `\\\\`,`\\\"`,`\\\"`? = escaped quote, then close: raw BSBS BSQ BSQ = BS BS BS Q BS Q.\\n\\n    So scanning rule in-string:\\n    - if raw[p]=BS and raw[p+1]=Q \u2192 that's inner `\\\"`?? But wait: could this BS be the second BS of an inner `\\\\` (raw BSBS)? Only if we mis-consumed earlier. If we always consume correctly, at any position p we're at an inner-char boundary. At a boundary, raw BS Q = inner `\\\"` (close), raw BS BS = inner `\\\\` = escape introducer: it consumes the NEXT inner char, whose raw form is: 1 char (if not `\\\"`/`\\\\`) \u2192 consume 3 total; or BS Q (if `\\\"`) \u2192 consume 4 total; or BS BS (if `\\\\`) \u2192 consume 4 total.\\n    \\n    So at boundary in-string:\\n    - raw BS Q \u2192 close string, p += 2.\\n    - raw BS BS:\\n       - raw[p+2]=BS and raw[p+3]=Q \u2192 inner `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 p += 4, stay in string.\\n       - raw[p+2]=BS and raw[p+3]=BS \u2192 inner `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 p += 4, stay in string.\\n       - else (raw[p+2] = normal char) \u2192 inner `\\\\X` \u2192 p += 3, stay in string.\\n    - else p += 1.\\n\\n    That's exact. And not-in-string (structural, inner level):\\n    - raw BS Q \u2192 inner string open \u2192 in_str = true, p += 2.\\n    - raw `{` \u2192 depth++, p++.\\n    - raw `}` \u2192 depth--, p++; if depth = 0 \u2192 done.\\n    - raw BS BS \u2192 shouldn't occur structurally; p += 2 for safety.\\n    - raw Q (bare) \u2192 outer-level quote (means we've left the arguments string / outer structure). For the arguments case this means overshoot (malformed). Just p++ and ignore (or could stop). I'll p++.\\n    - else p++.\\n\\n    Hmm \u2014 wait, one more consideration: the case where extract_balanced is used structurally (cases 1, 2, 4: tool_calls [, function {, output [). There the scanning is at the outer level: strings delimited by bare Q, escapes inside strings are BS + any. E.g., the chat body: `\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...}\\\"}]`. Scanning for `[`...`]` at outer level: strings are `\\\"c1\\\"`, `\\\"function\\\"`, `\\\"arguments\\\"`, and the big string `\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. Outer rules:\\n    - not in string: Q \u2192 enter string; open/close chars count; \\n    - in string: BS \u2192 skip next; Q \u2192 exit string.\\n\\n    Note the arguments string at outer level: `\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 in-string, BS Q \u2192 skipped; the `'{'` inside \u2014 we're in string \u2192 skipped. \\n\\n    So two scanners needed: outer-level (for split_entries, and extract_balanced when the open char is structural) and inner-level (for extract_balanced on escaped string values like arguments).\\n\\n    Can extract_balanced auto-detect? After finding the key, we FIND the first open char. If the char immediately before the open char is... For the arguments case: raw before the open `{` is BS (i.e., `\\\\\\\"{`). For structural cases: raw before `{` is `:` (possibly with spaces). Heuristic: if raw[openpos-1] = BS \u2192 inner mode; else outer mode. That's reliable for our usage: arguments values always come as `\\\\\\\"` + escaped JSON starting with `{`. Hmm, but what if arguments is empty or not an object? Then no `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` within... FIND FIRST `{` might find a later structural `{` \u2014 edge case, same behavior as before.\\n\\n    Alternatively, make extract_balanced always use a *combined* scanner that handles both: enter string on bare Q OR on BS Q?? Let's test: combined rule: not in_str: Q \u2192 in_str; BS Q \u2192 in_str; in_str: BS \u2192 skip 2 (generic); Q \u2192 exit. \\n\\n    Outer case: `...:\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...` \u2014 at `\\\"arguments\\\"`: Q \u2192 in_str; chars `arguments`; Q \u2192 exit; `:`; Q \u2192 in_str (the big string); then BS \u2192 skip Q; ... `{` inside string? skipped since in_str. String ends at the final Q. Works.\\n\\n    Inner case (starting after key, scanning from the open `{` which is inside the outer string): raw: BS Q `{` BS Q s o u r c e BS Q : BS Q ... We start at `{` with in_str = false. `{` \u2192 depth 1. Then BS Q \u2192 in_str = true (treat as string open). Then `s o u r c e`: chars; then BS Q: in_str + BS \u2192 skip 2 (the Q) \u2014 but that BS Q is the *close* of the inner string `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"`! With generic \\\"in_str + BS \u2192 skip 2\\\", we'd skip the closing quote and stay in string through `: ` then BS Q (open of value string) skipped too... we'd remain in string until a bare Q. Bare Q occurs at the outer string's closing quote (after the args object's `}`). Then exit string. Then `,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 Q \u2192 in_str ... then `}` at the end: in_str! not counted. Ugh \u2014 combined generic fails.\\n\\n    So: no clean unification; use mode detection via preceding BS. Fine \u2014 but careful: `split_entries` operates at outer level only. And extract_balanced: mode by raw[open-1] = BS \u2192 inner mode; else outer mode.\\n\\n    Hmm wait, actually for inner mode starting position: we start AT the open char `{` which is preceded by BS (the `\\\\\\\"` before `{` \u2014 that BS was \\\"consumed\\\" as part of entering... we start scanning at the open char itself with in_str=false, depth=1. The BS Q before it is ignored. Good.\\n\\n    Let me also double check inner mode with the ACTUAL failing example. Body (responses):\\n\\n    ```\\n    {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_x. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n    ```\\n\\n    split_entries (outer mode) on the output array `[entry1,entry2]`:\\n    - `{` depth 1. Strings: \\\"type\\\",\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\",\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\",\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\" each Q\u2192in, Q\u2192out. Then the big string: Q \u2192 in_str; content: BS Q { BS Q s...: BS Q C L A S S ... `'{` `'` `.` ... BS Q ... all skipped; ends at bare Q. Then `,\\\"status\\\"` strings; `:` `\\\"completed\\\"`; then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 entry1 ends correctly! Then entry2 similarly. Both entries split correctly. \\n\\n    Then parse_calls_responses on entry1: extract_balanced(key='arguments'): finds `\\\"arguments\\\"`, then FIND FIRST `{` in lv_rest \u2192 the `{` after `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 preceded by BS \u2192 inner mode. Scan from `{`: depth 1. Next BS Q \u2192 in_str. `source` ... BS Q \u2192 out. `:` BS Q \u2192 in. `CLASS ... '{'. ...` \u2014 inside string, the `{` skipped! Then BS Q (raw for close of source string) \u2192 out. Wait \u2014 in inner mode, in_str + BS Q \u2192 close (rule). Let me re-verify with rule table: in_str at boundary, raw BS Q \u2192 close. Yes. Then `,` BS Q \u2192 open `name`... etc. Then after `ZCL_X` string closes (BS Q), we hit `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 end. rv_ = `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ...\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}`. Then unescape \u2192 `{\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ...\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}`. PERFECT.\\n\\n    And entry2 parsed \u2192 second call recovered. \\n\\n    Also need to fix the chat path similarly: parse_calls_chat does extract_balanced(key='tool_calls', open='[') on the body (outer mode \u2014 fine), split_entries (outer mode \u2014 fixed), extract_balanced(key='arguments') inner mode \u2014 fixed, extract_balanced(key='function', open='{') \u2014 structural outer mode; the preceding char is `:` \u2192 outer mode. Good.\\n\\n    And in build_input_items / zif chat, extract_str with PCRE handles strings correctly-ish (`(?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*`). But note: for the chat `tool_calls` content stored... fine.\\n\\n    Now also `extract_balanced` is used for 'output' with `[`: outer mode.\\n\\n    Edge: what about `FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open IN lv_rest` in inner case \u2014 could it find a `{` inside a string before the arguments' `{`? Between the key and the value there's only `:\\\"` or `:\\\\\\\"`. Fine.\\n\\nNow let me also check `split_entries` current implementation for clamping (defensive) and rewrite with string awareness, keeping clamps.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. Write a red unit test. Where do tests for these classes live? \\\"Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\\\" The bug report mentions an existing test ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR \u2014 probably in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses. Let me read the current testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (read_object shows main include; testclasses is separate \u2014 the tool says read_object reads \\\"main include, definition+implementation\\\"; maybe testclasses included? It returned only main. I need to see the test include to add tests. The read tool doesn't have an include param... The description: \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation).\\\" So I can't read testclasses? Hmm, but write_source can write testclasses. Risky to overwrite unseen tests (ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR lives somewhere \u2014 likely in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses). If I overwrite the testclasses include without seeing it, I'd delete ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR \u2014 but the task says leave it as is. Maybe read_object on the class returns main only; maybe the test classes are visible via reading? Let me try read_object and see... I already read it; no testclasses shown. Hmm.\\n\\n    Where should the new test go? Options: testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parse_calls_responses is there) or of ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (extract_balanced/split_entries). The reproduction with the full body incl. `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` belongs naturally to ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP testclasses (parse_calls_responses). \\n\\n    I need to know what's in those testclasses includes already. Since read_object can't fetch them... maybe it can? The description says main include. Let me try anyway \u2014 maybe the environment returns both. Actually, maybe I should just try read_object with the class name and check output \u2014 already did for both classes; no test code shown. So I cannot read existing testclasses. Then writing testclasses blindly would clobber existing tests (ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR). ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is \\\"already red on this system\\\" \u2014 where? Probably in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL's testclasses (escape with emoji pair). If I write my new test into ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP testclasses and that include already has tests, I'd clobber them too.\\n\\n    Hmm. Maybe the testclasses includes are separate objects readable via read_object with a special name? Not per the schema. Risk: overwriting existing tests. The task explicitly says one existing test ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is red \u2014 implying it will still be there when I run tests. If I clobber it by writing a fresh testclasses include for JSON_UTIL, the instruction \\\"leave it as it is\\\" is violated and run_tests counts would change.\\n\\n    Safest: put my new tests in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP's testclasses include. But if that include already contains tests... clobbering them would be bad. Hmm.\\n\\n    Wait \u2014 maybe read_object DOES return testclasses for classes that have them, and these two classes simply have none yet? The description says \\\"(main include, definition+implementation)\\\" \u2014 ambiguous. The JSON_UTIL read result ended with ENDCLASS. without any test class. If there were tests, maybe they'd appear. But ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR must live somewhere. Let me think: escape/emoji \u2014 could be in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses. The read didn't show it. So read_object doesn't show testclasses. \\n\\n    Alternative: maybe there's a demo/test class like ZCL_PIA_DEMO mentioned in the tool description example. Not helpful.\\n\\n    Decision: write the reproduction test into the testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. If that include already had tests, my write replaces them \u2014 risk. Hmm, but is there any way to detect? After writing and activating, run_tests will report counts; if previously there were tests, I can't know. \\n\\n    Actually \u2014 think about ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR: which class's local tests would contain it? It tests `escape` presumably \u2192 ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL. The user says \\\"One existing test, ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR, is already red on this system... leave it as it is.\\\" This is a hint about which test RUN will show: when I run tests for the class I modify. If I modify JSON_UTIL and run its tests, I'd see ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR red among them. That suggests the user expects me to possibly work in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL and see that test. The mention is a warning: when you run tests on this class, you'll see 1 red \u2014 ignore it, don't \\\"fix\\\" or remove it.\\n\\n    So the intended flow: I'll write tests in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses?? But then I'd overwrite ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR unless I include it. I can't read it to reproduce it exactly... I could write my own version of an ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR test, but the user said leave it as it is \u2014 i.e., don't touch it. If I rewrite the include without knowing its content, I destroy it.\\n\\n    Alternatively put the new tests in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parse level) \u2014 the pure reproduction of the reported symptom (two calls, first has `'{'` in source, args polluted with `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`, second call lost) fits parse_calls_responses perfectly, and that class owns the parsing entry points. If LLM_HTTP has existing local tests, I'd clobber them. Risk either way.\\n\\n    Hmm, wait \u2014 maybe read_object returns the testclasses include too when it exists, i.e. the output for JSON_UTIL/LLM_HTTP lacking tests means they have none?? Then where is ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR? Maybe in another class that tests JSON_UTIL, like a ZCL_PIA_15_T_* tool class or the executor. E.g., a tool that escapes content (write_source escapes source into JSON) \u2014 ZCL_PIA_15_T_WRITE? Or session store. Can't know without reading.\\n\\n    Let me probe: read a couple of likely classes to find where ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR lives and to see whether read_object includes testclasses. Candidates: ZCL_PIA_15_T_WRITE? Registry lists tools ZCL_PIA_15_T_*. Let me read ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY to get exact tool names, and maybe the executor. Actually simpler: the phrase \\\"One existing test ... is already red on this system because of a known runtime issue of open-steamgate\\\" \u2014 tests run via run_tests(name) for \\\"the class whose local ABAP Unit tests\\\" \u2014 I'll run tests for the classes I touch. If I touch only JSON_UTIL and LLM_HTTP, and ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR lives elsewhere, I'd never see it. The user warns me so that IF I see it red, I don't chase it. It's most likely in one of the two classes I'll touch \u2014 JSON_UTIL most probably (escape lives there).\\n\\n    So the intended workflow probably: add my test to ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL's testclasses include \u2014 but to preserve ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR I'd need its source. UNLESS read_object gives me testclasses when present. The tool result I got shows none \u2014 but maybe because these classes have no testclasses yet and ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is in yet another class. Let me read a few more classes to locate it: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR, ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY. Hmm, reading many classes costs turns; I have unlimited turns though (\\\"Work on it on your own, turn after turn\\\").\\n\\n    Let me read ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tool names) and ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR quickly \u2014 the executor might have tests with escape emoji. Actually \u2014 hmm, the most probable place for ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR: the class whose main code is `escape` = JSON_UTIL. Its testclasses include exists but read_object didn't show it. I think read_object shows only the main include. \\n\\n    New idea: I can *append* tests safely?? write_source requires FULL source. No append. So any testclasses write is destructive unless I know current content.\\n\\n    Safest path that satisfies \\\"write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red\\\": put the test in a class whose testclasses include I can establish is empty/nonexistent. How to establish? If write_source to testclasses of LLM_HTTP with my tests, then run_tests(LLM_HTTP) \u2014 if there were pre-existing tests, they're gone (bad but unknown). \\n\\n    Alternatively, first run_tests on LLM_HTTP and JSON_UTIL BEFORE writing anything! That reveals current test counts and names (failures listed with names). If run_tests reports \\\"0 tests\\\" for a class, its testclasses include is empty \u2192 safe to write. And if JSON_UTIL shows ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR red, I know it's there and must preserve it \u2014 but I still can't read its source... though failure output gives expected/actual/line, not full source. Hmm. If ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is in JSON_UTIL and I must not clobber it, then I'd rather put my tests in LLM_HTTP if that include is empty.\\n\\n    Plan for this turn: call run_tests for ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL and ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (independent \u2192 same block). Based on results, decide where to write the red test.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_2026100719575578bf18ea19f74aa3\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by inspecting the current test situation of the two classes most likely involved (`ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL` for the brace scanner, `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP` for call parsing), so I know where existing tests (like `ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR`) live and where I can safely add a new one.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_2026100719575578bf18ea19f74aa3\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011320115723432\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011320115723432\",\"name\":\"run_tests\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011320115723431\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011320115723431\",\"name\":\"run_tests\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6561,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6528},\"output_tokens\":12924,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"r