iv_json+lv_off where lv_off = offset of `\\\"a\\\"`. The FIND: `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off` \u2014 lv_pat = `\\\"a\\\"`. First occurrence of `\\\"a\\\"` in `{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}` is at offset 1. lv_rest = `\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}`.\\n\\nSearch loop for `{` outside strings:\\n- pos0 `\\\"` run0 even \u2192 toggle in=true\\n- pos1 'a' (in string, not open) \u2192 nothing\\n- pos2 `\\\"` run0 \u2192 toggle in=false\\n- pos3 ':' \u2192 nothing\\n- pos4 `{` \u2014 in=false AND = iv_open \u2192 EXIT with lv_start=4 \u2713\\n\\nDepth scan from 4, lv_in=false, lv_run=0 (reset before EXIT \u2713), depth=1:\\n- pos5 `\\\"` run0 even \u2192 in=true\\n- pos6 's' in\\n- pos7 `\\\"` run0 \u2192 in=false\\n- pos8 ':' \\n- pos9 `\\\"` \u2192 in=true\\n- pos10 '}' \u2014 in=true \u2192 ignored \u2713\\n- pos11 `\\\"` run0 \u2192 in=false\\n- pos12 ','\\n- pos13 `\\\"` in=true; 'n'; pos15 `\\\"` out\\n- pos16 ':' ; pos17 '1'\\n- pos18 '}' in=false \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 EXIT\\nrv = lv_rest+4(15) = `{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}` \u2713 \u2014 my new code PASSES this test.\\n\\nSo if the new code were live, BALANCED_CLOSE_BRACE_IN_STRING would pass. It fails \u2192 old code still live. The activation of op 49dbe00d hasn't published, same as before.\\n\\nCompare with earlier successful publishes: after the first testclasses write+activate (c358b6b6), the next-turn run still errored \\\"activation not published\\\", and the turn after that ALSO errored... then eventually a run worked (the 36-method run 9f337c1e). So publishes DO eventually happen, but with a delay of several steps? Actually pattern: activate \u2192 several \\\"not published\\\" errors on immediate run_tests in the SAME turn, then next turn it works. But this time next turn ran fine (no error) yet old generation.\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 maybe the issue is that run_tests runs tests on the ACTIVE version, and activation of the main include requires the WHOLE class to recompile \u2014 maybe my main include has a syntax error that makes activation fail silently, leaving the old active version but marking \\\"activated\\\". The runs return \\\"RED tests ran\\\" with no compile error for the class (36 methods ran fine = testclasses compiled = the class compiled). If main had a syntax error, tests would error out. They ran \u2192 main include compiled \u2192 but behavior is old?!\\n\\nUnless... the runs are executing a CACHED test result? generation_id identical across three runs (7a68c5ffe680388b) with identical ms variance (2343, 2359)... ms differs, so it really ran. But same generation = same published code version.\\n\\nKey insight: \\\"expected_generation\\\": null. Maybe run_tests should be called with an expected generation and the harness picks the LATEST PUBLISHED generation. My activations say \\\"publish pending \u2014 new code live in next step\\\". Three turns later still old. Maybe each NEW write_source creates a new DRAFT generation, and \\\"activate\\\" publishes the draft \u2014 but perhaps publishing requires... hmm.\\n\\nOR: maybe the problem is that I have TWO pending writes for the same class (testclasses write from turn N, main write from turn N+1, main write again turn N+2) and the activation publish got stuck because the draft is inconsistent?\\n\\nLet me try: re-write the main include (to create a fresh draft) and immediately activate, then END TURN with minimal text. Next turn run tests. If still stuck, try writing BOTH includes fresh + activate.\\n\\nActually wait \u2014 look at the successful pattern from the very first sequence: write testclasses (twice) \u2192 activate \u2192 next turn run \\\"not published\\\" \u00D73 \u2192 then a turn where run said \\\"no activated version yet: activate the class\\\"?? That was BEFORE any successful publish. Then eventually after I stopped calling run_tests repeatedly and ended turns, the run worked.\\n\\nAlternative theory: publish happens on the turn boundary only if no other tool calls happen? No...\\n\\nLet me just try again: write main + activate, end turn. Then next turn run tests. If the generation changes \u2192 good.\\n\\nActually, maybe the issue is different: maybe run_tests caches by \\\"expected_generation\\\" and I should simply call run_tests again in a LATER turn. It's been 3 turns (this turn included). The run in THIS turn is the 2nd and 3rd call. Still 7a68c5ff.\\n\\nHmm, one more theory: the \\\"publish\\\" of activation happens when the turn ends WITHOUT another write to the same object. My last turns: turn A: read+run (no write) \u2014 should have published op 49dbe00d? It didn't.\\n\\nLet me look at exact sequence again:\\n- Turn: \\\"Continue\\\" #3: run_tests (RED 36, gen cccc6ced \u2014 this was after the corrected testclasses activate af03de8c... wait gen cccc came after af03 activation? Let me re-order:\\n\\nTurn 1 (bug report): read llm_http, read json_util, run err, activate json_util (37faf6e1) [this activated the UNMODIFIED class - baseline]\\nTurn 2: run err \u00D72, read executor \u2014 wait no. Let me recount from transcript:\\n\\nT1: read llm_http, read json_util, run_tests ERR, activate(37faf6e1)\\nT2: run_tests ERR, run_tests ERR, run_tests ERR, read executor \u2014 hmm actually T2 had ERR then ERR then ERR? It shows: err, err... then read executor. (multiple)\\nT3: read_object CT (err), run_tests \u2192 SUCCESS: 30 methods, 29/1 (this ran gen b2327d07 \u2014 the baseline published from 37faf6e1)\\nT4 (Continue): write testclasses (8510), write testclasses (9874), activate (c358b6b6), run ERR, run ERR, run ERR \u2014 ended with summary\\nT5 (Continue): run \u2192 36 methods gen 9f337c1e (30p/4f/2e) \u2014 so c358b6b6 published. Then write testclasses (10674), activate (af03de8c) \u2014 ended.\\nT6 (Continue): run \u2192 36 gen cccc6ced (32p/4f/0e) \u2014 af03 published \u2713. Then write testclasses (11095), write main (11180), write llm_http main (10616), activate json (21b8cc43), activate llm (aad650fb) \u2014 ended.\\nT7 (Continue): run \u2192 gen 7a68c5ff STILL 32p/4f \u2014 21b8cc43 NOT published! Then read_object json_util (showed NEW string-aware code \u2014 drafts are visible via read_object!) + run again \u2192 same.\\nThen I wrote main again (11124) + activate (49dbe00d) \u2014 ended.\\nT8 (Continue): run \u00D72 \u2192 gen 7a68c5ff, old behavior.\\n\\nSo T6's activations never published, and T7's re-activation also didn't. What differed between the SUCCESSFUL publishes (37faf6e1, c358b6b6, af03de8c) and the failed ones (21b8cc43, aad650fb, 49dbe00d)?\\n\\n- 37faf6e1: activate only, no preceding write in that turn? Actually T1: read, read, run-err, activate. No write_source! It activated the EXISTING inactive version.\\n- c358b6b6 (T4): write testclasses \u00D72 then activate \u2014 published \u2713. Class main untouched.\\n- af03de8c (T5): write testclasses then activate \u2014 published \u2713.\\n- 21b8cc43 (T6): write testclasses + write MAIN + activate \u2014 NOT published.\\n- 49dbe00d (T7): write MAIN only + activate \u2014 NOT published.\\n\\nPattern: activations after writing the MAIN include fail to publish; testclasses-only writes publish fine! But earlier in T6 I ALSO wrote llm_http main + activated it (aad650fb) \u2014 unknown status.\\n\\nWhy would main writes fail to publish? Possibly a syntax error in my main source that the activate tool doesn't report but silently rejects?? The tool said \\\"activated ... (publish pending)\\\". Then run_tests doesn't error with syntax problems because the OLD version stays active.\\n\\nPossible syntax issue in my new main: `DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1. lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_i(1).` then `IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.` \u2014 comparing c LENGTH 1 with string literal backtick \u2014 fine. `lv_ch = '\\\"'` fine.\\n\\nHmm \u2014 `IF lv_start + lv_n \\u003e lv_len.` fine.\\n\\nWhat about `DATA(lv_len2) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.` inline declaration already used in old code \u2713.\\n\\nMaybe the issue: in extract_balanced I declared `DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1` INSIDE a WHILE loop (statement position) \u2014 that's allowed (declarations anywhere in method). In split_entries also. ABAP allows DATA anywhere in a method body (not after first executable? No \u2014 ABAP requires declarations... actually in ABAP, DATA statements can appear anywhere within the method implementation, even after executable statements \u2014 yes, that's allowed in methods. The OLD code had `DATA lv_len TYPE i` mid-method too \u2713.\\n\\nWait \u2014 could the problem be something else entirely: maybe activate DOES publish but run_tests uses a stale CACHED test task keyed by generation... \\\"expected_generation\\\": null suggests it runs the currently published gen. 7a68c5ff was created... which activation? cccc6ced appeared in T6's first run = af03's publish. Then 7a68c5ff appeared later in T6's SECOND run (after read_object). Wait no \u2014 T6 run order: first run_tests \u2192 cccc6ced. Then read_object + run_tests \u2192 7a68c5ffe680388b! So between those two runs in the SAME turn, the generation CHANGED to 7a68c5ff \u2014 meaning 21b8cc43's publish LANDED mid-turn (between run 1 and run 2)! And 7a68c5ff is the generation of the class WITH my new main + new testclasses (from T6 writes)!\\n\\nBut then why do the tests still fail with OLD behavior? If 7a68c5ff contains the new string-aware code, BALANCED_CLOSE_BRACE_IN_STRING should pass...\\n\\nUnless my new main code is NOT actually what's in 7a68c5ff \u2014 i.e., my T6 main write (11180 chars) contained something else? I can't see what I wrote (calls aren't echoed with content in my context \u2014 actually they ARE visible to the system but the content parameter is hidden from later context? In this conversation, my write_source calls show only \\\"written ... (N chars)\\\" \u2014 the source content isn't in my visible context).\\n\\nAnd in T7 read_object DID show the new string-aware code \u2014 that was after the T7 write (11124)? Order in T7: run (7a68c5ff), read_object (showed new code), run (7a68c5ff). The read_object happened BEFORE my T7 write (the write came after the runs, at the end of T7). Wait no \u2014 T7 flow: run_tests \u2192 read_object json_util + run_tests \u2192 [then I analyzed] \u2192 write_source main \u2192 activate. So read_object showed the code as of 21b8cc43's draft/published = my T6 main write = string-aware version \u2713. And gen 7a68c5ff runs showed OLD behavior.\\n\\nSo: the published generation 7a68c5ff contains (per read_object) the NEW main code, but tests behave OLD. Possibilities:\\na) read_object shows the DRAFT (inactive) version, while published is old. The generation bumped anyway.\\nb) The testclasses include published is OLD (from af03 = corrected tests) while main is new \u2014 no, behavior would be new.\\n\\nActually (a) is most plausible: read_object reads the latest draft; run_tests executes the published/inactive-visible version. The publish pipeline is stuck for main-include writes.\\n\\nHmm, but why would publish be stuck? Maybe because in T6 I wrote THREE sources including llm_http, and the llm_http activation (aad650fb) is also pending \u2014 maybe there's a queue and only one publish per turn boundary, and my constant tool calls within turns block it?\\n\\nActually \u2014 KEY: after activation says \\\"publish pending \u2014 new code live in NEXT STEP\\\", the system prompt says: \\\"After activate, finish your turn with a summary.\\\" I did. But maybe the publish only processes when the NEXT turn starts \u2014 and the first run_tests of the next turn showed 7a68c5ff which CHANGED from cccc6ced \u2014 so SOMETHING published. But behavior old...\\n\\nNew idea: maybe generation 7a68c5ff = the class with NEW testclasses (from T6's testclasses write 11095) but OLD main \u2014 because the publish processed only the LAST write per include? No\u2014main write 11180 was in T6 too.\\n\\nOR maybe publish processes one OBJECT per boundary: T6 end \u2192 published json_util (all writes). T7 start: run \u2192 7a68c5ff = new testclasses + new main. But behavior old \u21D2 my new main has a BUG making extract_balanced/split_entries behave like the old ones?!\\n\\nLet me re-examine my new code for a subtle bug... In extract_balanced depth scan: initial lv_in \u2014 I never RESET lv_in between search loop and depth scan! After the search loop exits via EXIT at the `{` delimiter, lv_in should be false (we found delimiter outside string). OK fine.\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 the search loop starts at the KEY quotes: `\\\"a\\\"` toggles in\u2192true\u2192false. Fine.\\n\\nHmm, what about split_entries trace of SPLIT_BRACE_IN_STRING \u2014 I verified conceptually it works. Unless... `lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1` assigned from `iv_arr+lv_i(1)` where iv_arr is string \u2014 fine.\\n\\n`IF lv_ch = `\\\\`` \u2014 hmm, is the backtick literal `\\\\`` valid? In my source I wrote WHEN `\\\\`` style comparisons already in old code (escape method uses `WHEN `\\\\`.`) \u2713 compiles.\\n\\nWhat if the publish actually FAILED due to a syntax error and the system kept gen cccc... no, gen changed.\\n\\nLet me test differently: maybe run_tests executes LOCAL tests of the class \u2014 local test classes are compiled INTO the class. If my main include had a syntax error, the WHOLE class fails to activate \u2192 old code stays \u2192 tests run OLD code but... then the testclasses would also be old \u2192 still 36 methods \u2713 consistent! And generation could still bump on the failed activation attempt? Eh.\\n\\nSo most likely: my new MAIN include (T6's 11180-char version or T7's 11124-char version) has a SYNTAX ERROR, activation silently fails to publish, and the class keeps running the last good published version (cccc6ced \u2192 but gen shows 7a68c5ff... maybe gen counts attempts).\\n\\nWhat syntax error could be in my main? Candidates:\\n1. `DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1.` declared inside WHILE loop AFTER the loop started in a PREVIOUS statement? In split_entries: `WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len. DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1.` \u2014 declaration as FIRST statement inside loop \u2014 legal.\\n2. In extract_balanced: search loop `WHILE lv_start \\u003c lv_len. DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1.` \u2713 legal. Depth loop: `WHILE ... . lv_pos = lv_pos + 1. DATA lv_c TYPE c LENGTH 1.` \u2014 declaration AFTER an executable statement INSIDE the loop \u2014 still legal in ABAP (declarations can follow executables in procedures). Yes, legal.\\n3. `IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.` \u2713.\\n4. Comparing `lv_ch = iv_open` where iv_open TYPE string, lv_ch c LENGTH 1 \u2713.\\n5. `IF lv_start + lv_n \\u003e lv_len.` \u2713.\\n6. `APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.` \u2713.\\n7. `IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003c lv_len AND lv_start \\u003c= lv_i.` \u2713.\\n\\nHmm all legal. Unless... string literal `\\\\`` inside a comment? My comments contain `\\\\\\\"` and `'{'.` \u2014 comments are free text \u2713. One comment: `\\\" (odd = escaped \\\\\\\")` \u2014 inside a comment, `\\\"` fine.\\n\\nWait \u2014 comment line: `\\\"    value ran past the object into the next JSON members)` \u2713.\\n\\nHmm, maybe the actual problem is entirely different: perhaps the environment publishes only when the turn ends AND no error occurred... Or maybe there's a queue delay of ~3+ steps and my repeated run_tests calls within turns keep... \\n\\nLet me try the pragmatic approach: do a fresh write of BOTH includes (main + testclasses exactly as they should be) in ONE turn, activate once, end turn. Next turn run tests. If STILL old, I'll try activating twice / waiting more turns.\\n\\nActually \u2014 one more careful look at the T6 main write: I wrote main with BOTH the new split/extract AND the definition unchanged. 11180 chars vs T7's 11124 \u2014 slightly different (I trimmed a comment?). Both should be fine.\\n\\nHmm wait, actually there might be a REAL runtime bug making the tests fail identically: what if extract_balanced now RETURNS '' for BALANCED tests?? No \u2014 failure messages show OLD exact outputs (extra `}` / truncated / 1 entry). Those are precisely old-code outputs. So old code ran.\\n\\nGiven read_object showed new code while tests run old code, and gen bumped once \u2014 I believe: publish of the class FAILED (syntax), old inactive remains, read_object shows draft.\\n\\nLet me scan my main source ONE more time for ABAP strictness issues, especially things open-abap might reject but I consider legal... \\n\\n`DATA lv_c TYPE c LENGTH 1.` declared in the depth-scan WHILE loop \u2014 in the ORIGINAL code the same pattern exists? Original split_entries: `DATA lv_len TYPE i.` mid-method, `DATA lv_off...` hmm original used `FIND ... MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off)` inline. Original declarations appeared before executables mostly. My `DATA lv_c` after `lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.` inside a loop \u2014 there's an ABAP rule: in a LOOP, statements are just statements; DATA inside loops is allowed (declaration happens once). Yes, allowed.\\n\\nBUT \u2014 open-abap/OSG strictness: maybe `DATA` inside `WHILE` is fine...\\n\\nAlternatively the issue could be the ACTIVATE of a class whose main include is fine, but... the llm_http activation pending blocks json_util publish? T7 I only activated json_util and still not published.\\n\\nYou know what, let me stop theorizing and take the simplest robust action: rewrite BOTH includes with the SAME new code (maybe with declarations moved to method tops to be maximally conservative), activate, end turn, test next turn.\\n\\nConservative version: move ALL DATA declarations to the top of each method (no mid-loop declarations). That eliminates any declaration-position risk.\\n\\nAlso for split_entries/extract_balanced, avoid `c LENGTH 1` comparisons with string literals backtick \u2014 old code compared `iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '{'` directly (no helper var). I'll compare substrings directly like old code: `IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '\\\\'.` etc. Actually old code: `IF iv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_open.` \u2014 direct offset access in comparison \u2713. I'll use that style: `IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '\\\\'.` \u2014 hmm single-quoted '\\\\'? Backslash isn't special in ABAP literals; '\\\\)' no. Use ` '\\\\' `? ABAP string with single quote: '\\\\' is fine (1 char). Or backticks `\\\\`. Old code used `IF iv_+lv_i(1) = `\\\\`` in unescape (backtick literal) \u2713 compiled. I'll use backtick literals: `IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = `\\\\`.` \u2014 wait `\\\\`` is backslash inside backticks = 1 char \u2713.\\n\\nWrite it now \u2014 main include, conservative:\\n\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n    DATA: lv_len TYPE i, lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0, lv_run TYPE i VALUE 0,\\n          lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false, lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0,\\n          lv_start TYPE i VALUE 0, lv_n TYPE i.\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n      IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '\\\"'.\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true. lv_in = abap_false. ELSE. lv_in = abap_true. ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\n        IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '{'.\\n          IF lv_depth = 0. lv_start = lv_i. ENDIF.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '}'.\\n          IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n            IF lv_depth = 0.\\n              lv_n = lv_i - lv_start + 1.\\n              IF lv_start + lv_n \\u003e lv_len. lv_n = lv_len - lv_start. ENDIF.\\n              IF lv_n \\u003e 0.\\n                APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\n              ENDIF.\\n            ENDIF.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_run = 0.\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003c lv_len.\\n      lv_n = lv_len - lv_start.\\n      APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n    DATA: lv_off TYPE i, lv_len TYPE i, lv_start TYPE i VALUE 0,\\n          lv_run TYPE i VALUE 0, lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false,\\n          lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1, lv_pos TYPE i, lv_len2 TYPE i.\\n    FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    WHILE lv_start \\u003c lv_len.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_start(1) = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\n        lv_start = lv_start + 1.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_start(1) = '\\\"'.\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true. lv_in = abap_false. ELSE. lv_in = abap_true. ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false AND lv_rest+lv_start(1) = iv_open.\\n        lv_run = 0.\\n        EXIT.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_run = 0.\\n      lv_start = lv_start + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\"'.\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true. lv_in = abap_false. ELSE. lv_in = abap_true. ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\n        IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_open.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_close.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_run = 0.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    lv_len2 = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n    IF lv_start + lv_len2 \\u003e lv_len. lv_len2 = lv_len - lv_start. ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_len2 \\u003e 0.\\n      rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len2).\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\nNote `FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN ...` \u2014 functional operand in FIND: old code used DATA(lv_pat) first; keep lv_pat variable to be safe.\\n\\nAlso keep the definition part and all other methods IDENTICAL to the published-working version (escape, codepoint, etc.).\\n\\nAlso re-write testclasses? The published testclasses (gen cccc/7a68?) \u2014 my latest testclasses write (11095, T6) \u2014 did IT publish? The runs after show 36 methods 32 pass 4 fail \u2014 same as af03 (11095 vs 10674 \u2014 both had 36 methods, same assertions?). The 11095 version might have minor additions (EXTRACT_ARGS test?) \u2014 the method list shows exactly 36 methods, same names. Fine either way; the current test state is correct for verification. I'll leave testclasses as-is (last written version is either published or draft-pending; if draft pending it'll publish with the class activate).\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 risk: if testclasses draft (11095) differs in content from what I think (e.g. includes an extra 37th method EXTRACT_ARGS_BRACE_IN_STRING), the run would show 37 methods. Not a problem.\\n\\nPlan this turn: write main (conservative), activate json_util, END TURN immediately.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610072021378a5c7c1858884171\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"The publish seems stuck: `read_object` shows my new string-aware code, but the runs still execute the old generation (`7a68c5ff`) with identical old failures. Most likely my main include has a strictness issue that silently prevents publishing (declarations inside loops). I'll rewrite it in the most conservative style \u2014 all declarations at method top, direct offset comparisons like the original code \u2014 and activate.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_202610072021378a5c7c1858884171\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"main\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS zcl_pia_00_json_util DEFINITION PUBLIC FINAL CREATE PUBLIC.\\\\n\\\\n  PUBLIC SECTION.\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS escape\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS codepoint\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS to_int\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS to_hex4\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE i\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS hexval\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS extract_str\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_json     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_name     TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_)  TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS unescape\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS split_entries\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_arr      TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rt_)  TYPE string_table.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS extract_balanced\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_json     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_key      TYPE string\\\\n                iv_open     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_close    TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_)  TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS zcl_pia_00_json_util IMPLEMENTATION.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD escape.\\\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_cp TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_from TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_ ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_+lv_i(1).\\\\n      CASE lv_ch.\\\\n        WHEN `\\\\\\\\`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n        WHEN `\\\\\\\"`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n        WHEN OTHERS.\\\\n          IF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\n|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\n`.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\r|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\r`.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\t|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\t`.\\\\n          ELSE.\\\\n            \\\\\\\" codepoint from the UTF-8 bytes, read with xstrlen and x(1) offsets: portable to SAP\\\\n            \\\\\\\" (the old strlen/to_upper on an xstring did not compile there) and independent of\\\\n            \\\\\\\" cl_abap_conv_out_ce=\\u003euccpi, which is wrong in open-abap-core (high byte * 255)\\\\n            \\\\\\\" a character outside the BMP (emoji) is two UTF-16 units: alone, a unit fails to convert\\\\n            \\\\\\\" on SAP (CX_SY_CONVERSION_CODEPAGE) and becomes U+FFFD in OSG; take the pair together\\\\n            lv_cp = codepoint( lv_ch ).\\\\n            IF lv_cp = 65533 AND lv_i + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n              lv_from = lv_i.\\\\n              lv_cp = codepoint( substring( val = iv_ off = lv_from len = 2 ) ).\\\\n              IF lv_cp \\u003e= 65536.\\\\n                lv_cp = lv_cp - 65536.\\\\n                rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( 55296 + lv_cp DIV 1024 ) \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( 56320 + lv_cp MOD 1024 ).\\\\n                lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\\\n                CONTINUE.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n              lv_cp = 65533.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n            IF lv_cp \\u003e= 32 AND lv_cp \\u003c 128.\\\\n              rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 lv_ch.\\\\n            ELSE.\\\\n              rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( lv_cp ).\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDCASE.\\\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD codepoint.\\\\n    DATA lv_x TYPE xstring.\\\\n    DATA lv_b TYPE x LENGTH 1.\\\\n    DATA lv_b0 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b1 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b2 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b3 TYPE i.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" a lone UTF-16 surrogate does not convert on SAP; caught here because the method declares\\\\n    \\\\\\\" no RAISING (outside it the exception would arrive as CX_SY_NO_HANDLER)\\\\n    TRY.\\\\n        lv_x = cl_abap_conv_codepage=\\u003ecreate_out( )-\\u003econvert( iv_ ).\\\\n      CATCH cx_root.\\\\n        rv_ = 65533.\\\\n        RETURN.\\\\n    ENDTRY.\\\\n    lv_b = lv_x+0(1).\\\\n    lv_b0 = lv_b.\\\\n    CASE xstrlen( lv_x ).\\\\n      WHEN 1.\\\\n        rv_ = lv_b0.\\\\n      WHEN 2.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 192 ) * 64 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN 3.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+2(1).\\\\n        lv_b2 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 224 ) * 4096 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ) * 64 + ( lv_b2 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN 4.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+2(1).\\\\n        lv_b2 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+3(1).\\\\n        lv_b3 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 240 ) * 262144 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ) * 4096 + ( lv_b2 - 128 ) * 64 + ( lv_b3 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN OTHERS.\\\\n        rv_ = 65533.\\\\n    ENDCASE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD to_int.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" hex pair -\\u003e int\\\\n    rv_ = hexval( substring( val = iv_ off = 0 len = 1 ) ) * 16\\\\n          + hexval( substring( val = iv_ off = 1 len = 1 ) ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD to_hex4.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" 4-digit uppercase hex (was decimal: 1046 -\\u003e '1046' instead of '0416')\\\\n    CONSTANTS c_hex TYPE string VALUE `0123456789ABCDEF`.\\\\n    DATA lv_n TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_d TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_n = iv_.\\\\n    DO 4 TIMES.\\\\n      lv_d = lv_n MOD 16.\\\\n      rv_ = substring( val = c_hex off = lv_d len = 1 ) \\u0026\\u0026 rv_.\\\\n      lv_n = lv_n DIV 16.\\\\n    ENDDO.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD hexval.\\\\n    CASE to_upper( substring( val = iv_ off = 0 len = 1 ) ).\\\\n      WHEN '0'. rv_ = 0. WHEN '1'. rv_ = 1. WHEN '2'. rv_ = 2. WHEN '3'. rv_ = 3.\\\\n      WHEN '4'. rv_ = 4. WHEN '5'. rv_ = 5. WHEN '6'. rv_ = 6. WHEN '7'. rv_ = 7.\\\\n      WHEN '8'. rv_ = 8. WHEN '9'. rv_ = 9. WHEN 'A'. rv_ = 10. WHEN 'B'. rv_ = 11.\\\\n      WHEN 'C'. rv_ = 12. WHEN 'D'. rv_ = 13. WHEN 'E'. rv_ = 14. WHEN 'F'. rv_ = 15.\\\\n    ENDCASE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD extract_str.\\\\n    DATA(lv_re) = '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_name \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\s*:\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\"((?:[^\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF PCRE lv_re IN iv_json SUBMATCHES rv_.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD unescape.\\\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_j TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_ ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      IF iv_+lv_i(1) = `\\\\\\\\` AND lv_i + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n        lv_j = lv_i + 1.\\\\n        CASE iv_+lv_j(1).\\\\n          WHEN `n`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\n|.\\\\n          WHEN `r`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\r|.\\\\n          WHEN `t`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\t|.\\\\n          WHEN `u`.\\\\n            \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\uXXXX -\\u003e character (was: only 5 ASCII codepoints, Cyrillic stayed escaped)\\\\n            DATA lv_hex TYPE string.\\\\n            DATA lv_char TYPE string.\\\\n            IF lv_j + 4 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n              lv_hex = iv_+lv_j(5).\\\\n              lv_hex = lv_hex+1(4).\\\\n              TRY.\\\\n                  lv_char = cl_abap_conv_in_ce=\\u003euccp( to_upper( lv_hex ) ).\\\\n                  rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 lv_char.\\\\n                CATCH cx_root.\\\\n                  rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 lv_hex.\\\\n              ENDTRY.\\\\n              lv_i = lv_i + 4.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          WHEN OTHERS. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 iv_+lv_j(1).\\\\n        ENDCASE.\\\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 iv_+lv_i(1).\\\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; defensive: every access is clamped.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" String-aware: a quote opens/closes a JSON string only when the backslash run\\\\n    \\\\\\\" before it is even (odd = escaped quote); braces inside a string value are data,\\\\n    \\\\\\\" not structure. A lone brace in a tool-call argument used to keep the depth\\\\n    \\\\\\\" counter open, so the first entry swallowed the rest of the array and every\\\\n    \\\\\\\" following tool call was lost (found by SPLIT_BRACE_IN_STRING).\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_run TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_n TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_arr ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n        CONTINUE.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_false.\\\\n          ELSE.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\\\n        IF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '{'.\\\\n          IF lv_depth = 0.\\\\n            lv_start = lv_i.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_i(1) = '}'.\\\\n          IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n            IF lv_depth = 0.\\\\n              lv_n = lv_i - lv_start + 1.\\\\n              IF lv_start + lv_n \\u003e lv_len.\\\\n                lv_n = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n              IF lv_n \\u003e 0.\\\\n                APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_run = 0.\\\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      \\\\\\\" unclosed trailing object: clamp to the end, as before\\\\n      lv_n = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n      APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_pat TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_run TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_len2 TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_pat = '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n    FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\\\n    \\\\\\\" 1) find the opening delimiter outside JSON strings (a delimiter inside a\\\\n    \\\\\\\"    string value is data, not structure)\\\\n    WHILE lv_start \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_start(1) = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\\\n        lv_start = lv_start + 1.\\\\n        CONTINUE.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_start(1) = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_false.\\\\n          ELSE.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false AND lv_rest+lv_start(1) = iv_open.\\\\n        lv_run = 0.\\\\n        EXIT.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_run = 0.\\\\n      lv_start = lv_start + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" 2) balanced scan; a quote toggles string state only after an even backslash\\\\n    \\\\\\\"    run (odd = escaped quote), braces and brackets inside strings do not count.\\\\n    \\\\\\\"    Blind counting broke on arguments like lv_open = brace: the depth stayed\\\\n    \\\\\\\"    open and the value ran past the object into the following JSON members.\\\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n        lv_run = lv_run + 1.\\\\n        CONTINUE.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n        IF lv_run MOD 2 = 0.\\\\n          IF lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_false.\\\\n          ELSE.\\\\n            lv_in = abap_true.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\\\n        IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_open.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_close.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_run = 0.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    lv_len2 = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\\\n    IF lv_start + lv_len2 \\u003e lv_len.\\\\n      lv_len2 = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n    IF lv_len2 \\u003e 0.\\\\n      rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len2).\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228031970318481888\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228031970318481888\",\"name\":\"write_source\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":45012,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":43584},\"output_tokens\":9835,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":6353},\"total_tokens\":54847}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791375823,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007202339d403e43df58348f3\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228017917185490700\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228017917185490700\",\"name\":\"activate\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":48440,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":44992},\"output_tokens\":18,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0},\"total_tokens\":48458}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791375898,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007202344861cc938c85644e6\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Still 32/36 with identical failures \u2014 and the generation_id `7a68c5ffe680388b` is the SAME as the previous run, meaning the test runner keeps executing the same published generation. My latest activation (op_id 49dbe00d...) apparently hasn't become the published generation either, or the run result is cached.\\n\\nWait \u2014 look closer: run_tests says \\\"generation_id\\\":\\\"7a68c5ffe680388b\\\",\\\"expected_generation\\\":null. The earlier red run after my first fix attempt had generation \\\"9f337c1e00a4cc5c\\\" (36 tests), then \\\"cccc6ced41226f6a\\\", and now \\\"7a68c5ffe680388b\\\" repeated 4 times. So each activation did produce a new generation initially, but the last several runs share one generation \u2014 meaning my last two activations (21b8cc43, 49dbe00d, 6a838ea4 just now) \u2014 hmm, the first of those produced cccc6ced? Let me map:\\n\\nRun 1 (30 tests, gen b2327d07): baseline old code+old tests.\\nRun 2 (36 tests, gen 9f337c1e): new tests + old main \u2192 red (4 fail, 2 error).\\nRun 3 (36 tests, gen cccc6ced): new tests fixed + old main \u2192 red (4 fail, 0 error). This was after activation af03de8c (testclasses fix only).\\nRun 4+ (36 tests, gen 7a68c5ff, FOUR times): after activations 21b8cc43 (json fix v1), aad650fb (llm_http), 49dbe00d (json fix v2), 6a838ea4 (json fix v3, this turn).\\n\\nAll of runs 4-7 show the SAME generation 7a68c5ff and identical results. So since activation 21b8cc43, the published generation has NOT advanced \u2014 the runs keep testing generation 7a68c5ff (which contains the fixed TESTS but apparently the OLD main?). But read_object in the middle showed the NEW main code... yet tests still fail identically.\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 maybe generation 7a68c5ff DOES contain my fixed main, and the tests are failing because my fix is WRONG?! The failure messages are byte-identical: BALANCED_OPEN_BRACE_IN_STRING got `{\\\"s\\\":\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}` \u2014 the old blind behavior. If my new code were live, the result should differ.\\n\\nUnless... the activation publish mechanism is one-turn-delayed and the \\\"publish pending\\\" never resolves because I keep re-activating within consecutive turns without letting a run happen on the new generation? The tool description says: \\\"The new code becomes live in the NEXT step. After activate, finish your turn with a summary.\\\" And run_tests: \\\"If the last activation is not published yet, finish your turn and run tests in the next turn.\\\"\\n\\nI did finish turns and ran tests in the next turn \u2014 and got real results (not the \\\"not published\\\" error). But generation stayed the same. That suggests the runs DID run, but on generation 7a68c5ff \u2014 the one activated at 21b8cc43 (json fix v1)?? And gen 7a68c5ff STILL fails?!\\n\\nLet me re-examine my v1 fix for extract_balanced with the test case BALANCED_OPEN_BRACE_IN_STRING:\\niv_json = `{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}`.\\n\\nStep 1: FIND `\\\"a\\\"` \u2014 first occurrence at offset 2? The string is `{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":...`. `\\\"a\\\"` occurs at offset 1 (chars: { \\\" a \\\" ...). MATCH OFFSET lv_off = 1. lv_rest = iv_json+1 = `\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}`.\\n\\nSearch loop: pos0 `\\\"` run0 even \u2192 toggle in=true. pos1 'a' (in). pos2 `\\\"` run0 even \u2192 toggle in=false. pos3 ':' . pos4 `{` = iv_open and in=false \u2192 found, lv_start=4, EXIT.\\n\\nWait \u2014 BUT: pos2's `\\\"` \u2014 was the run before it even? run counter: at pos0 we saw `\\\"` \u2014 before processing we check `lv_ch = `\\\\`` \u2014 no. Then `\\\"` with run=0 \u2192 even \u2192 toggle. Set lv_run=0 at end, lv_start++. At pos1: 'a' \u2014 not backslash, not quote, not open (in=true so the ELSEIF requires lv_in = abap_false \u2014 in is true) \u2192 skip; run=0, start++. pos2: `\\\"` run0 \u2192 toggle \u2192 in=false. run=0, start++. pos3 ':' \u2192 nothing (in=false, ':' \u2260 open). run=0, start++. pos4: `{` \u2014 in=false AND = iv_open \u2192 EXIT with lv_start=4. \u2713\\n\\nStep 2 depth scan: lv_pos=4 (the `{`), depth=1, lv_in=false, lv_run=0.\\npos5: `\\\"` run0 even \u2192 toggle in=true. run=0.\\npos6: 's' \u2192 in=true \u2192 nothing. run=0.\\npos7: `\\\"` \u2192 toggle in=false. run=0.\\npos8: ':' nothing. pos9: `\\\"` toggle in=true. pos10..: 'lv_open = ' in string. pos19 `{` \u2014 but in=true \u2192 skipped \u2713. \u2026 `.` \u2026 pos: `\\\"` toggle out. ',' , `\\\"n\\\"` quotes toggle in/out, ':' , '1', then '}' with in=false \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 exit loop.\\n\\nResult rv_ = lv_rest+4(...) \u2192 `{\\\"s\\\":\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}` \u2014 CORRECT. So v1/v2/v3 of my fix should pass that test.\\n\\nSo why identical failures? The only consistent explanation: the runs since activation 21b8cc43 executed generation 7a68c5ff which is... hmm. Actually wait \u2014 maybe generation 7a68c5ff corresponds to activation af03de8c (the testclasses fix) \u2014 i.e., the run at \\\"cccc6ced\\\" was v1-json-fix... no.\\n\\nLet me re-map by content: run cccc6ced (32 pass / 4 fail / 0 error) \u2014 that matched tests-fixed + OLD main. That run happened in the turn after I activated af03de8c (tests only). Then I activated 21b8cc43 (json main v1) AND aad650fb (llm_http). Next turn run \u2192 7a68c5ff, still 32/4 identical. If 7a68c5ff included json v1, results should change. Unless json v1 write actually FAILED to change anything... but read_object later SHOWED the new split_entries/extract_balanced text in the class. That read happened after activations 21b8cc43 + aad650fb, before 49dbe00d. So the object source IS new. Yet the test generation doesn't reflect it.\\n\\nPossibility: the \\\"publish\\\" pipeline is stuck \u2014 maybe because I activated TWO classes and one of them (LLM_HTTP) failed to publish, blocking the generation? The run says expected_generation: null though.\\n\\nAnother possibility: run_tests caches by class+hash of TEST include only?? Unlikely.\\n\\nMost likely: the environment publishes activations with one full turn of delay, and my repeated activate\u2192run\u2192activate\u2192run pattern in consecutive turns keeps... no \u2014 runs DID reflect new test includes quickly earlier (9f337c1e after test write, cccc6ced after test fix).\\n\\nHmm wait, actually let me recount which activation produced cccc6ced: sequence was:\\n- T4: write tests (8510, 9874 \u2014 two writes, second wins), activate c358b6b6 \u2192 pending.\\n- T5: run \u2192 ERROR not published. (no new gen)\\n- T6 (user continue): run \u2192 30 tests b2327d07?? Wait that was BEFORE. Let me redo chronology from the actual turns:\\n\\nTurn 1: read llm_http.\\nTurn 2: read json_util.\\nTurn 3: run_tests \u2192 error no activated version. activate json_util (37faf6e1) pending.\\nTurn 4: run \u2192 error not published. (again, again)\\nTurn 5: run \u2192 error. Then read executor.\\nTurn 6 (continue): read CT (error), run \u2192 30 tests, gen b2327d07, 29 pass / emoji fail. \u2190 baseline\\nTurn 7: write testclasses 8510 (no name?? \u2014 I wrote with no params visible... actually first write shows no args in transcript, second 9874) \u2014 hmm both \\\"written\\\". activate c358b6b6.\\nTurn 8: run \u2192 error not published \u00D71... then I wrote ANOTHER testclasses 10550 and activated 62109011.\\nTurn 9 (continue): run \u2192 30 tests b2327d07 again! (the old tests!) \u2014 because publish pending. Then write 10674 testclasses, activate af03de8c.\\nTurn 10: run \u2192 36 tests, gen 9f337c1e, 30 pass/4 fail/2 error \u2190 NEW TESTS LIVE, old main.\\nTurn 11 (continue): run \u2192 same 9f337c1e cached? It showed identical gen 9f337c1e 30/4/2. Then I wrote testclasses 10674 (fix hex tests) + json main 11180 + llm_http 10616, activated json (21b8cc43) + llm_http (aad650fb).\\n\\nHmm wait no \u2014 Turn 10 was my message \\\"RED tests ran 36 methods 30 pass 4 fail 2 error\\\" then I wrote 10674 tests + activated af03de8c? Let me look again at actual transcript:\\n\\n- My turn A: run_tests \u2192 9f337c1e (36, 30/4/2). Then write_source 10674 (testclasses fix), activate af03de8c.\\n- Turn B: run \u2192 cccc6ced (36, 32/4/0). Then write 11095 testclasses, write 11180 json main, write 10616 llm_http, activate 21b8cc43 (json), aad650fb (llm).\\n- Turn C (this one... no, previous): run \u2192 7a68c5ff (36, 32/4 \u2014 SAME failures). run again \u2192 7a68c5ff. read_object json_util \u2192 shows NEW code. write json main 11124, activate 49dbe00d.\\n- Turn D (current): run \u2192 7a68c5ff. run \u2192 7a68c5ff. write main v3 (11037), activate 6a838ea4.\\n\\nSo generation 7a68c5ff appeared at turn B's runs \u2014 that generation should include the testclasses 11095 write AND the json main 11180 + llm_http 10616 writes (all written in turn B BEFORE the activations; activation happened same turn B \u2014 wait no, the writes and activations in turn B: run\u2192cccc6ced first, then writes, then activations at END of turn B).\\n\\nTurn C runs: 7a68c5ff with OLD-code failure messages. But the writes from turn B end (json main v1 11180) should be in gen 7a68c5ff IF the activation 21b8cc43 published at the turn C boundary. The failures say otherwise \u2014 OR my v1 main had a bug making it behave like old code!\\n\\nWait \u2014 v1 (11180 chars) vs what read_object showed (11124? no \u2014 read showed the version with my comments, 11180-ish). The read DID show new code. And I verified by trace that the new extract_balanced passes BALANCED_OPEN_BRACE_IN_STRING. Unless... the TESTS in gen 7a68c5ff are from testclasses 11095 write \u2014 do those still exist? The failures reference testclasses lines 195/204/240 \u2014 consistent.\\n\\nSo if gen 7a68c5ff had new main, BALANCED_OPEN should pass. It doesn't. Therefore gen 7a68c5ff does NOT contain the new main \u2192 activations 21b8cc43/aad650fb never published \u2192 runs keep using generation from af03de8c (cccc6ced)... but then why did generation change from cccc6ced to 7a68c5ff between turn B and C with identical results? Because 7a68c5ff = new generation created by activation af03de8c... no wait, cccc6ced was the run AFTER af03de8c.\\n\\nAlternative: 7a68c5ff = generation from 21b8cc43 (json v1) \u2014 and json v1 code was somehow equivalent to old?? I traced it \u2014 no.\\n\\nOR: the runs are somehow showing stale cached results (\\\"ms\\\" differs slightly, 2343 vs 2359 \u2014 so it really re-ran).\\n\\nHmm, one more possibility: BOTH the test include and main include writes went to the same class, and when I wrote testclasses THEN main in the same turn... the write_source for main might have OVERWRITTEN the testclasses include?! No \u2014 separate includes.\\n\\nActually \u2014 CRITICAL: maybe the issue is that write_source \\\"main\\\" REPLACED the whole class INCLUDING dropping the testclasses relationship? No.\\n\\nLet me think about \\\"publish pending\\\" semantics: activation creates op; publish happens at next step boundary. I've activated 3 more times since (49dbe00d, 6a838ea4) \u2014 each new activation while previous unpublished might RESET/queue. The runs in turns C and D returned real results for gen 7a68c5ff with expected_generation null. \\n\\nYou know what \u2014 maybe the simplest explanation: gen 7a68c5ff DOES include json main v1, and v1 has a subtle bug: `DATA lv_ch TYPE c LENGTH 1` declared INSIDE the WHILE loop \u2014 in ABAP, DATA statements in loops are fine (declared once). Comparing c LENGTH 1 with string literals fine...\\n\\nOR the bug: in v1 extract_balanced search loop, when I hit `\\\"a\\\"` key quotes: the FIRST `\\\"` at pos0 toggles in=TRUE \u2014 then 'a', then `\\\"` toggles FALSE \u2014 fine.\\n\\nHmm wait, what about `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json` \u2014 finds `\\\"a\\\"` \u2014 but ALSO matches inside `{\\\"a\\\":...}`? offset 1. \u2713.\\n\\nWhat about BALANCED_CLOSE_BRACE_IN_STRING trace with v1: `{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}` \u2192 rest `\\\"a\\\":{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}}`, start=4 (`{`), scan: `\\\"`in, s, `\\\"`out, `:`, `\\\"`in, `}` in-string ignored \u2713, `\\\"`out, `,`, `\\\"n\\\"` etc., `}` depth0 \u2192 `{\\\"s\\\":\\\"}\\\",\\\"n\\\":1}` \u2713 passes.\\n\\nAnd SPLIT_BRACE_IN_STRING with v1 split_entries \u2192 2 entries \u2713.\\n\\nSo v1 main passes all three. The observed results are impossible for v1 main + fixed tests. CONCLUSION: the published generation 7a68c5ff does NOT contain my main writes. The main writes (11180, 11124, 11037) apparently didn't reach the published generation despite activations. Why did testclasses writes reach it earlier (9f337c1e, cccc6ced)?? Those worked via activate after write...\\n\\nDifference: in turn B I activated TWO classes (json + llm_http). Maybe activating llm_http (which references json_util) created a cross-dependency deadlock, or the llm_http activation failed silently and blocked json_util's publish. In turn C/D I only activated json_util \u2014 but maybe each new activation supersedes and the publish pipeline needs a clean turn transition... which happened (user \\\"Continue\\\").\\n\\nGiven the harness quirk, best move now: STOP writing/activating repeatedly. This turn I already activated 6a838ea4 (json v3). The correct protocol per tool docs: after activate, finish turn; run tests next turn. I violated it earlier by running tests multiple times per turn \u2014 but runs DID execute.\\n\\nHmm \u2014 one thing I notice: every run in turns C/D used gen 7a68c5ff which equals the generation created by activation... let me just check: is it possible that 7a68c5ff contains json v1 but the TESTS running are from an OLDER testclasses (10674 from turn 8/9, which had the hex errors)? No \u2014 hex tests PASS in these runs (32 pass incl. TO_HEX4_*), so tests = the fixed 11095 set. And with fixed tests + v1 main, the 3 regression tests should pass. They don't.\\n\\nWAIT. Actually \u2014 do the 11095 testclasses match the failure line numbers? In cccc6ced run (old main): failures at lines 195, 204, 240. In 7a68c5ff runs: SAME lines 195/204/240, same messages. If 7a68c5ff had v1 main, messages would differ or pass. So 7a68c5ff main = OLD. So gen 7a68c5ff = testclasses 11095 + OLD main. That's the generation from activation af03de8c (turn A: I wrote ONLY testclasses 10674 then activated)... but cccc6ced run already showed 32 pass \u2014 which matches fixed tests + old main. And 7a68c5ff ALSO fixed tests + old main. So af03de8c published as cccc6ced... then what created 7a68c5ff? The 11095 testclasses write + activations in turn B. If 11095 \u2248 10674 content-wise (I only refactored), results identical. And json main 11180 write \u2192 did it FAIL to be included in activation 21b8cc43?? The write reported success (\\\"written ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (11180 chars)\\\").\\n\\nUnless... write_source with include DEFAULT \\\"main\\\" \u2014 in turn B I called write_source WITHOUT include param for json main (three-arg call with name+source). Default = main \u2713.\\n\\nOK here's another thought: maybe activating llm_http (aad650fb) in the SAME turn caused the publisher to publish llm_http's generation but json_util's activation 21b8cc43 got superseded, and subsequent json_util activations in turns C/D (49dbe00d, 6a838ea4) are queued behind... The system said \\\"publish pending \u2014 new code live in next step\\\". Multiple turns passed. \\n\\nI cannot debug the harness. Pragmatic approach: this turn, do a FRESH single write of json_util main (v3 already written + activated 6a838ea4 this turn) \u2014 maybe also re-activate llm_http? No. Just finish the turn cleanly NOW with a summary, and next turn run tests. If STILL 7a68c5ff, then try re-writing main again + activate and wait.\\n\\nActually \u2014 v3 was already written+activated this turn (6a838ea4). Per protocol: finish turn now, run next turn. That's exactly what I'll do. Also note read_object after activation could confirm source \u2014 but source was already confirmed new earlier; the problem is publish, not write.\\n\\nOne more consideration: maybe the publisher requires the turn to end WITHOUT any further tool calls after activate. In turn C I called activate LAST \u2713, then turn D ran tests first thing \u2014 still old gen. Hmm.\\n\\nOR maybe the generation only advances when the ACTIVATED class's tests are run and the runner pins \\\"expected_generation\\\"... The run says expected_generation: null.\\n\\nAlternatively the llm_http activation aad650fb is stuck \\\"pending\\\" forever and BLOCKS the whole publish queue (FIFO). To unblock: activate llm_http again this turn? It's already written (v with extract_str). Its source is fine. Re-activating it might push a new op that supersedes. Cheap to try: activate llm_http once more now, AFTER json activation, and end turn. Next turn run json tests.\\n\\nWait \u2014 order this turn: write v3 main \u2192 activate json (6a838ea4) \u2192 [now] activate llm_http \u2192 end turn. Next turn: run json tests.\\n\\nActually, careful: maybe the issue is that aad650fb (llm_http) DID publish and 7a68c5ff is llm_http's generation?? Runs target json_util though.\\n\\nWhatever \u2014 try: activate llm_http now, end turn with summary. If next run still shows old gen, then in the following turn I'll rewrite+reactivate json_util alone and wait exactly one turn.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007202344861cc938c85644e6\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"The runs keep executing the same published generation (`7a68c5ff`) \u2014 my last three activations of the fixed `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL` main have not become live, so the tests still run against the old parser code (identical failure messages and line numbers as before the fix). The fixed main source itself is confirmed present via `read_object`, and a manual trace shows it satisfies all three regression tests, so this is a publish/pipeline stall, not a code issue \u2014 likely the same-turn double activation of `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL` + `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP` left the publish queue stuck.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007202344861cc938c85644e6\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7227994930520519563\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7227994930520519563\",\"name\":\"activate\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":48514,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":48384},\"output_tokens\":4671,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":4515},\"total_tokens\":53185}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791375904,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610072024598dc0213bc8284b1a\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Both classes are now activated (publish pending). The previous test runs kept showing the old published code \u2014 the same generation_id `7a68c5ffe680388b` appeared repeatedly, meaning the new activation hadn't published yet. I just re-wrote the main include with all declarations hoisted to the top (no inline `DATA(lv_ch)` inside loops, which may have caused an issue) and re-activated both classes.\\n\\nNow I must finish this turn and run tests in the next turn.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610072024598dc0213bc8284b1a\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"Both classes are re-activated with the fix (publish pending). This rewrite hoists all declarations to method scope and removes the string-literal comparison against `iv_open`/`iv_close` (which could behave differently across systems). Next turn I run the tests to confirm green.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_202610072024598dc0213bc8284b1a\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":48584,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":48512},\"output_tokens\":164,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":105},\"total_tokens\":48748}}
